insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ismail$tk$, 'en', 8, $tk$Ismail (Ishmael)$tk$, $tk$إسماعيل$tk$, $tk$True to his promise, builder of the Kaaba with his father$tk$, $tk$Ismail, peace be upon him, is the son of Ibrahim, peace be upon him. Ibrahim had asked his Lord for a righteous child, and he thanked Allah for granting him Ismail and Ishaq in his old age.

The Qur'an reports the prayer of Ibrahim saying that he had settled some of his descendants in a valley without crops, near the Sacred House, so that they would establish prayer. A long hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (no. 3364) relates how Ibrahim left Ismail, still a baby, with his mother Hajar in that empty valley of Mecca, how she ran between the hills of Safa and Marwa looking for water, and how the spring of Zamzam appeared. Pilgrims still walk between these two hills in memory of her trust in Allah.

The Qur'an then tells of a great test. Ibrahim saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son, and he asked him what he thought. The son, described as gentle and forbearing, answered that his father should do as he was commanded, and that he would find him, if Allah willed, among the patient. When both had submitted, Allah called to Ibrahim that he had fulfilled the vision, and He ransomed the son with a great sacrifice. The Qur'an does not name the son in this passage; most Muslim scholars hold that it was Ismail.

Father and son then worked together on a task that still marks the life of Muslims. Allah charged Ibrahim and Ismail to purify His House for those who walk around it, stay there in worship, bow and prostrate. Together they raised the foundations of the Kaaba, asking Allah to accept this from them, to make them and their descendants people who submit to Him, and to send to the people of that land a messenger from among themselves. Muslims see in the coming of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, a descendant of Ismail, the answer to this prayer.

The Qur'an sums up the character of Ismail in a few words. He was true to his promise, a messenger and a prophet. He used to enjoin prayer and charity on his people, and his Lord was pleased with him. Elsewhere he is counted among the patient and among those whom Allah favoured.$tk$, $tk$Keep your promises, even small ones, and care about the faith of those close to you. Trust in Allah grows through patience in hard moments.$tk$, $tk$["2:125-129","19:54-55","37:101-107","14:37-39","21:85-86"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ishaq$tk$, 'en', 9, $tk$Ishaq (Isaac)$tk$, $tk$إسحاق$tk$, $tk$The good news given to Ibrahim in his old age$tk$, $tk$Ishaq, peace be upon him, is the second son of Ibrahim, peace be upon him. The Qur'an tells the story of his birth in some detail, but says little about the rest of his life.

One day, guests came to Ibrahim and greeted him with peace. He returned their greeting and, generous as he was, quickly brought them a roasted calf. But the guests did not reach for the food. Ibrahim felt uneasy. They told him not to be afraid: they were angels, sent to the people of Lut, and they brought him good news of a son full of knowledge.

Ibrahim's wife was standing nearby. The Qur'an says that she was given the good news of Ishaq and, after Ishaq, of Yaqub. She was astonished: how could she have a child when she was an old woman who had never been able to bear children, and her husband was an old man? The angels answered: was she surprised at the decree of Allah? They wished the mercy and blessings of Allah upon the people of the house.

The promise came true. Ibrahim praised Allah for giving him Ismail and Ishaq in his old age, and declared that his Lord hears every prayer.

The Qur'an describes Ishaq as a prophet from among the righteous, and says that Allah blessed Ibrahim and Ishaq. It adds that Allah made Ishaq and Yaqub leaders who guided by His command, and inspired them to do good deeds, to establish prayer and to give charity; they were devoted worshippers of Allah. When Yaqub was close to death, his sons promised to worship the God of his fathers, Ibrahim, Ismail and Ishaq, one God.

Beyond this, the Qur'an gives no account of Ishaq's mission or of the events of his life. The many details found in other sources are not confirmed by the Qur'an or by sound hadith, so we do not repeat them here.

From Ishaq came Yaqub, and from Yaqub a long line of prophets among the Children of Israel, including Yusuf, Musa, Dawud and Isa, peace be upon them all. The birth of Ishaq shows that Allah keeps His promises in His own time, even when every visible cause seems to say that it is too late.$tk$, $tk$Never think it is too late for a prayer to be answered. Generosity towards guests and trust in Allah open doors that seemed closed.$tk$, $tk$["11:69-73","37:112-113","51:24-30","14:39","21:72-73"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yaqub$tk$, 'en', 10, $tk$Yaqub (Jacob)$tk$, $tk$يعقوب$tk$, $tk$The father whose patience was beautiful$tk$, $tk$Yaqub, peace be upon him, is the son of Ishaq and the grandson of Ibrahim, peace be upon them. He is also known as Israil, and his descendants are called the Children of Israel. The Qur'an speaks of him mainly as a father, in the story of his son Yusuf.

When young Yusuf told him about a dream in which eleven stars, the sun and the moon bowed to him, Yaqub understood that Allah had chosen this child. He advised him not to tell his brothers, for fear that jealousy would push them to plot against him.

The brothers did plot. They asked to take Yusuf out with them. Yaqub said that it saddened him to let him go and that he feared a wolf might eat him. In the evening they came back in tears, with false blood on Yusuf's shirt. Yaqub did not believe their story. He told them that their own souls had led them to do something, and he chose beautiful patience, asking Allah alone for help.

Years passed. When a second son was kept in Egypt, the pain came back. Yaqub turned away and grieved for Yusuf, and his eyes turned white with sorrow, which he kept inside. His sons blamed him for still thinking about Yusuf. He answered that he complained of his sadness and grief only to Allah, and that he knew from Allah what they did not know.

Then he gave them this advice: go and look for Yusuf and his brother, and do not despair of the mercy of Allah.

His hope was rewarded. Yusuf, now a high official in Egypt, sent his shirt to be placed over his father's face. As the caravan left Egypt, Yaqub said that he could sense the scent of Yusuf. When the shirt was laid on his face, he could see again. His sons asked him to pray for their forgiveness, and he promised to do so. The whole family was reunited in Egypt.

The Qur'an also records Yaqub's last concern. As death approached, he asked his sons what they would worship after him. They answered that they would worship his God and the God of his fathers, Ibrahim, Ismail and Ishaq, one God.$tk$, $tk$Sadness is not a lack of faith: bring your pain to Allah and keep hoping. Never despair of His mercy.$tk$, $tk$["2:132-133","12:4-6","12:83-87","12:93-100"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yusuf$tk$, 'en', 11, $tk$Yusuf (Joseph)$tk$, $tk$يوسف$tk$, $tk$The truthful one, from the well to the storehouses of Egypt$tk$, $tk$The story of Yusuf, peace be upon him, fills a whole surah of the Qur'an. As a boy, he dreamed that eleven stars, the sun and the moon bowed to him. His father Yaqub, peace be upon him, told him to keep the dream from his brothers.

The brothers were jealous of their father's love for Yusuf. They took him out and threw him to the bottom of a well, then told their father that a wolf had eaten him. Travellers passing by drew Yusuf out of the well, and he was sold in Egypt for a few coins.

He grew up in the house of a high Egyptian official. When he became a man, the wife of this official tried to seduce him. Yusuf sought refuge in Allah and ran to the door; she tore his shirt from behind, which proved his innocence. Yet he was still sent to prison. He had even said that prison was dearer to him than what he was being called to do.

In prison, Yusuf was known for his goodness. He interpreted the dreams of two fellow prisoners and took the opportunity to speak to them about the one God. One of them was freed, but forgot to mention Yusuf to his master, and Yusuf remained in prison for several years.

Then the king had a dream: seven fat cows eaten by seven lean ones, and seven green ears of grain with others that were dry. Yusuf explained it: seven years of good harvests, then seven hard years, so the grain had to be stored. Before leaving prison, he asked that his innocence be established, and the wife of the official admitted the truth. Yusuf asked to be put in charge of the storehouses of the land, and he managed them carefully.

When famine came, his brothers travelled to Egypt for food. Yusuf recognised them, but they did not recognise him. After several journeys, he revealed who he was. They admitted their wrong. He answered that there was no blame on them that day and that he hoped Allah would forgive them.

Yusuf then brought his parents and all his family to Egypt. His childhood dream had come true. He ended with a prayer, asking his Lord to let him die in submission to Him and to join him with the righteous.$tk$, $tk$Stay honest and pure whether you are at the bottom or at the top. When you finally have power over those who hurt you, forgiveness is the noblest choice.$tk$, $tk$["12:4-21","12:23-35","12:36-57","12:88-101"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ayyub$tk$, 'en', 12, $tk$Ayyub (Job)$tk$, $tk$أيوب$tk$, $tk$The model of patience$tk$, $tk$Ayyub, peace be upon him, is the prophet whose name has become a symbol of patience. The Qur'an counts him among the prophets who received revelation and among the descendants of Ibrahim whom Allah guided.

The Qur'an tells his story in a few verses, without many details. Ayyub was struck by a severe trial that touched his health and his family. The Qur'an does not describe his illness, nor does it say how long it lasted. Many stories have been told about this, some of them exaggerated and unworthy of a prophet; they are not established and we leave them aside.

What the Qur'an has kept is the way Ayyub spoke to his Lord. He did not complain to people, and he did not accuse Allah. He simply called out that hardship had touched him, and that Allah is the Most Merciful of the merciful. In another passage, he says that Satan had afflicted him with distress and suffering. His prayer contains no demand: he describes his condition, he praises the mercy of his Lord, and he leaves the rest to Him.

Allah answered him. He told him to strike the ground with his foot: a spring of cool water appeared, to wash with and to drink. His suffering was removed. Allah gave him back his family and as many more with them, as a mercy from Him and a reminder for those who worship and for people of understanding.

The Qur'an also mentions that Ayyub had made an oath during his trial. Allah showed him a gentle way to keep his word without doing harm: to take a bundle of grass in his hand and strike with it. The Qur'an does not explain the circumstances of this oath in detail.

The passage ends with the praise of Allah Himself: He found Ayyub patient, an excellent servant, who always turned back to Him.

The patience of Ayyub was not silence or the absence of pain. He felt his suffering and said so, but he said it to Allah, with respect and hope. His story has comforted sick and grieving people for centuries, because it shows that a trial is not a sign of rejection by Allah, and that relief can come from where no one expects it.$tk$, $tk$In hardship, speak to Allah about your pain without losing respect or hope. Patience does not mean feeling nothing; it means staying faithful while it hurts.$tk$, $tk$["21:83-84","38:41-44","4:163","6:84"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$shuayb$tk$, 'en', 13, $tk$Shuayb$tk$, $tk$شعيب$tk$, $tk$The prophet of honest trade$tk$, $tk$Shuayb, peace be upon him, was sent to the people of Madyan. The Qur'an calls him their brother, for he was one of them. It also mentions his preaching to the people of the Thicket; scholars differ on whether these were the same people as Madyan or a neighbouring group.

The people of Madyan were prosperous, but their trade was built on cheating. They gave less than was due when they measured and weighed, and they deprived people of what belonged to them. They also sat on the roads to threaten people and to turn the believers away from the path of Allah.

Shuayb called them first to worship Allah alone. Then he spoke to them about their daily business: give full measure and full weight with justice, do not cheat people of their goods, and do not spread corruption on earth after it has been set in order. He told them that what Allah leaves them as honest profit is better for them. He reminded them that they had once been few and that Allah had multiplied them.

Their answer was mocking. Did his prayer command him that they should give up what their fathers worshipped, or stop doing what they pleased with their own money? Shuayb answered with words that have remained famous: he did not want to do himself what he forbade them; he only wanted to put things right as far as he could, and his success depended on Allah alone, in whom he trusted.

He warned them not to let their opposition to him bring upon them a fate like that of the peoples of Nuh, Hud, Salih or Lut. He invited them to ask forgiveness, saying that his Lord is merciful and loving.

The leaders turned to threats. They said that they did not understand much of what he said, that they saw him as weak among them, and that without his family they would have stoned him. They threatened to expel him and the believers unless they returned to their religion.

The punishment came. The Qur'an speaks of an earthquake and a mighty blast that left them lifeless in their homes, and of the punishment of the day of the dark cloud. Shuayb and those who believed with him were saved by the mercy of Allah.$tk$, $tk$Faith shows in the way we buy, sell and work: give everyone their due. Practise what you ask of others.$tk$, $tk$["7:85-93","11:84-95","26:176-191","29:36-37"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$musa$tk$, 'en', 14, $tk$Musa (Moses)$tk$, $tk$موسى$tk$, $tk$The one to whom Allah spoke$tk$, $tk$Musa, peace be upon him, is the prophet most often mentioned in the Qur'an. He was born in Egypt at a time when Pharaoh oppressed the Children of Israel and had their newborn sons killed. Allah inspired the mother of Musa to place her baby in the river, promising to return him to her. Pharaoh's household picked him up, and Pharaoh's wife asked that he be spared. Since the baby refused every nurse, he was given back to his own mother to be fed.

As a young man, Musa struck an Egyptian who was fighting with a man of his people, and the man died. Musa had not meant to kill him; he regretted it at once and asked Allah for forgiveness. He fled to Madyan. There he helped two women to water their flock, then sat down in the shade and prayed that he was in need of any good Allah would send him. Their father welcomed him, gave him one of his daughters in marriage, and Musa worked for him for years.

On his way back with his family, he saw a fire near Mount Tur. There Allah spoke to him, made him His messenger and gave him signs: his staff turned into a snake and his hand shone white. Musa asked that his brother Harun be sent with him. Allah told them to go to Pharaoh and to speak to him gently.

Pharaoh refused to listen and summoned his magicians. But when they saw the staff of Musa swallow their tricks, they recognised the truth and declared their faith, in spite of Pharaoh's threats.

Allah then ordered Musa to leave by night with his people. Pharaoh chased them with his army as far as the sea. The people panicked, but Musa answered that his Lord was with him and would guide him. He struck the sea with his staff; it parted, and they crossed. Pharaoh and his soldiers were drowned.

Musa then went to meet his Lord for forty nights and received the Tablets. In his absence, some of his people began to worship a calf made from their jewellery. Musa came back angry and grieved, and called them to repent.

For the rest of his life, Musa led his people with patience, carrying the Torah, a guidance and a light.$tk$, $tk$Nobody is too weak to be chosen for something great, and no tyrant is too strong to fall. When you feel trapped, remember that Allah is with those who trust Him.$tk$, $tk$["28:3-35","20:9-79","26:52-68","7:142-145","2:51-54"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$harun$tk$, 'en', 15, $tk$Harun (Aaron)$tk$, $tk$هارون$tk$, $tk$The brother and helper of Musa$tk$, $tk$Harun, peace be upon him, is the brother of Musa, peace be upon him. In the Qur'an his story is always linked to that of Musa: he is the prophet who was given as a support to another prophet.

When Allah spoke to Musa and sent him to Pharaoh, Musa measured the weight of the mission. He asked his Lord to open his heart, to make his task easy and to loosen his tongue so that people would understand him. Then he asked for a helper from his own family: Harun, his brother, to strengthen him and to share his task. Musa said that Harun was more fluent than he was. Allah granted the request, and the Qur'an says that, out of His mercy, Allah gave Musa his brother Harun as a prophet.

The two brothers went to Pharaoh together. Allah had told them to speak to him gently, in the hope that he would remember or fear. They asked him to let the Children of Israel go and to stop tormenting them. When the magicians of Pharaoh saw the truth, they declared that they believed in the Lord of Harun and Musa.

After the crossing of the sea, Musa went to meet his Lord for forty nights. Before leaving, he asked Harun to take his place among the people, to act rightly and not to follow the way of those who spread corruption.

During this absence came the test of the calf. A man the Qur'an calls the Samiri led the people astray with a calf made from their jewellery. The Qur'an makes it clear that Harun did not take part in this. He told them that they were being tested by it, that their Lord was the Most Merciful, and that they should follow him and obey him. They refused to listen until Musa returned.

When Musa came back, angry and grieved, he seized his brother by the head. Harun answered him tenderly, calling him son of my mother. He explained that the people had overpowered him and had almost killed him, and that he had feared to divide the Children of Israel. Musa then asked Allah to forgive him and his brother and to admit them both into His mercy.

Elsewhere the Qur'an counts Harun among the prophets who were guided and rewarded for doing good.$tk$, $tk$Supporting someone else's mission is as noble as leading it. In a conflict with someone close, answer with gentleness and explain yourself calmly.$tk$, $tk$["20:29-36","28:34-35","20:90-94","7:150-151","19:53"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$dhul-kifl$tk$, 'en', 16, $tk$Dhul-Kifl$tk$, $tk$ذو الكفل$tk$, $tk$Counted among the patient and the best$tk$, $tk$Dhul-Kifl, peace be upon him, is one of the figures about whom the Qur'an tells us the least. He is mentioned only twice, each time in a single verse and alongside other prophets.

In Surah al-Anbiya, he is named with Ismail and Idris. The Qur'an says that all of them were among the patient, that Allah admitted them into His mercy, and that they were among the righteous. In Surah Sad, he is named with Ismail and Al-Yasa, and the Qur'an says that all of them were among the best.

That is everything the Qur'an says. It does not tell us where or when he lived, to which people he was sent, or what he did. No sound hadith adds any clear detail about his life either.

His name itself has been discussed. In Arabic, Dhul-Kifl means something like the one with the portion, or the one who took on a commitment. Some commentators have suggested that he was given this name because he took on a heavy duty and fulfilled it faithfully, but this is an explanation of the word, not an established account. Others have tried to identify him with figures known from other traditions; these suggestions are guesses, and none of them rests on firm evidence.

Most scholars consider that he was a prophet, because the Qur'an names him and praises him among prophets. A few scholars have held that he was a righteous man rather than a prophet. In both views, he is a servant whom Allah Himself has praised, and that is the greatest honour.

It is worth noticing which qualities the Qur'an chose to mention. The first is patience: the ability to keep doing what is right over a long time, without giving up when things are hard. The second is righteousness. The third is to be counted among the best, in the company of prophets like Ismail and Al-Yasa.

So it is better to say honestly that we do not know the details of his life than to fill the silence with legends. The Qur'an does not always teach through long stories. Sometimes a name and a word of praise are enough to remind us that Allah knows His faithful servants, including those whose story no one has written down.$tk$, $tk$You do not need to be famous to be precious in the sight of Allah. Patience and faithfulness to your commitments are noticed by Him, even when nobody else sees them.$tk$, $tk$["21:85-86","38:48"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$dawud$tk$, 'en', 17, $tk$Dawud (David)$tk$, $tk$داود$tk$, $tk$Prophet, king and devoted worshipper$tk$, $tk$Dawud, peace be upon him, appears in the Qur'an for the first time on a battlefield. The Children of Israel, led by their king Talut, faced the army of Jalut. The believers were few, but they asked Allah for patience and firmness. They defeated their enemies by the permission of Allah, and Dawud killed Jalut. Allah then gave him kingship and wisdom and taught him what He willed.

Dawud became both a prophet and a king. Allah gave him the Zabur, the Psalms. The Qur'an describes him as a servant of great strength who constantly turned back to his Lord. When he glorified Allah, the mountains and the birds repeated the praises with him, in the evening and at sunrise.

Allah also made iron soft for him and taught him to make coats of armour with well-measured links, to protect people in battle. Although he was a king, a hadith tells us that Dawud ate only from what he earned by the work of his own hands (Sahih al-Bukhari, no. 2072).

His kingdom was made strong, and he was given wisdom and sound judgement in speech. But the Qur'an also tells of a moment when he was tested. Two men in dispute came into his prayer chamber and asked him to judge between them. Dawud gave his answer, then understood that Allah was testing him. He asked his Lord for forgiveness, fell down in prostration and repented, and Allah forgave him. The Qur'an gives no further details about this test, and the stories that have been added to it are not worthy of a prophet.

After this, Allah addressed him directly: He had made him a successor on earth, so he had to judge between people with truth and not follow his own desires.

The Qur'an also mentions a case about a field damaged at night by the sheep of some people. Dawud and his son Sulayman both gave a judgement, and Allah gave Sulayman the better understanding of the case, while both of them had received wisdom and knowledge.

Dawud thus brings together things we often think of as separate: power and humility, the skill of a craftsman and the devotion of a worshipper.$tk$, $tk$Whatever responsibility you hold, be fair and do not let your own wishes decide for you. When you realise you were wrong, turn back to Allah straight away.$tk$, $tk$["2:251","38:17-26","21:78-80","34:10-11","17:55"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$sulayman$tk$, 'en', 18, $tk$Sulayman (Solomon)$tk$, $tk$سليمان$tk$, $tk$The king who answered every gift with gratitude$tk$, $tk$Sulayman, peace be upon him, was the son of Dawud, peace be upon him, and inherited from him prophethood and kingship. The Qur'an calls him an excellent servant who constantly turned back to Allah. He asked his Lord to forgive him and to grant him a kingdom such as no one after him would have.

Allah answered his prayer. He put the wind at his service, blowing by his command wherever he directed it. Jinn worked for him as builders and divers and made what he wanted. He was taught the language of the birds, and his army was made up of jinn, men and birds, lined up in ranks.

One day this army came to a valley of ants. An ant warned the others to go into their homes so that Sulayman and his soldiers would not crush them without noticing. Sulayman understood her and smiled. Instead of feeling proud, he asked Allah to help him be grateful for His favours and to do good deeds that please Him.

Another time, he noticed that the hoopoe was missing. The bird returned with news from Sheba: a queen ruled there, with a great throne, and she and her people bowed to the sun instead of Allah. Sulayman sent her a letter, which began with the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, inviting them to come to him in submission.

The queen consulted her advisers and chose caution. She sent a gift, which Sulayman refused: what Allah had given him was better. Before she arrived, her throne was brought to him in the blink of an eye by one who had knowledge of the Scripture. Seeing it in front of him, Sulayman said that this was a favour from his Lord, to test whether he would be grateful or ungrateful.

When the queen entered his palace, she took the floor of smooth glass for water. She understood that she had been mistaken about many things, and declared that she submitted, with Sulayman, to Allah, the Lord of the worlds.

The Qur'an also tells of his death. He died leaning on his staff, and the jinn kept on working, unaware of it, until a small creature of the earth ate through the staff and he fell. They then understood that they did not know the unseen.$tk$, $tk$The more you receive, the more grateful and humble you should be. Every ability is a test of what you will do with it.$tk$, $tk$["27:15-44","21:81-82","34:12-14","38:30-40"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$ilyas$tk$, 'en', 19, $tk$Ilyas (Elijah)$tk$, $tk$إلياس$tk$, $tk$The messenger who called his people away from Baal$tk$, $tk$Ilyas, peace be upon him, is mentioned in two passages of the Qur'an. The first, in Surah al-An'am, names him in a list of prophets, together with Zakariyya, Yahya and Isa, and says that all of them were among the righteous. The second, in Surah as-Saffat, gives the short account of his mission.

The Qur'an says that Ilyas was one of the messengers. He spoke to his people and asked them whether they would not fear Allah. They worshipped an idol called Baal. Ilyas asked them how they could call upon Baal and abandon the best of creators: Allah, their Lord and the Lord of their forefathers.

His argument was simple and clear. The one who created them and created their ancestors before them is the only one who deserves to be worshipped. An idol has created nothing, and to turn to it is to forget the One to whom they owe their existence.

The Qur'an then tells us that his people rejected him. Because of this they will be brought to account, except the sincere servants of Allah. This means that some people, even if they were few, did listen to him and remained faithful.

The passage ends with a tribute. Allah left for Ilyas an honourable mention among later generations and sent peace upon him. He says that this is how He rewards those who do good, and that Ilyas was one of His believing servants.

The Qur'an gives no other details. It does not name the people of Ilyas, the land where he lived or the time of his mission, and it does not describe how his life ended. Commentators generally place him among the prophets sent to the Children of Israel, and other traditions tell long stories about him, but those accounts are not confirmed by the Qur'an or by sound hadith. We therefore keep to what is certain.

What is certain is already rich in meaning. Ilyas stood up in front of his people and asked them a question that every generation can ask itself: what do we put in the place of our Creator? The idol may no longer be a statue; it can be anything that we obey or love more than Allah. Many centuries later, his name is still spoken with a greeting of peace.$tk$, $tk$Ask yourself honestly what takes the first place in your heart. Speaking the truth is worth it even if only a few people listen.$tk$, $tk$["37:123-132","6:85"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$al-yasa$tk$, 'en', 20, $tk$Al-Yasa (Elisha)$tk$, $tk$اليسع$tk$, $tk$Counted among the chosen and the best$tk$, $tk$Al-Yasa, peace be upon him, is a prophet whom the Qur'an mentions only twice, and each time very briefly. There is no story about him in the Qur'an: only his name, placed among other prophets, and words of praise.

The first mention is in Surah al-An'am. After speaking of Ibrahim, Allah lists many of the prophets He guided. Among them come Ismail, Al-Yasa, Yunus and Lut, and the Qur'an says that Allah favoured all of them above all people. A little further on, the passage tells the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ that these are the ones whom Allah guided, and that he should follow their guidance.

The second mention is in Surah Sad. Allah tells the Prophet ﷺ to remember Ismail, Al-Yasa and Dhul-Kifl, and says that all of them were among the best.

This is all that the Qur'an says about him. We are not told to which people he was sent, when he lived, what he said to his people or how they answered him. No sound hadith gives an account of his life. Commentators generally place him among the prophets of the Children of Israel, after Ilyas, and other traditions tell stories about him. Since these stories are not confirmed by the Qur'an or by sound hadith, we do not present them as facts.

It is better to accept this silence than to fill it. The Qur'an itself says elsewhere that there are messengers whose stories Allah has told and others whose stories He has not told. Believing in all the prophets does not require knowing the details of each life.

Still, the two verses teach us something. Al-Yasa is described as chosen, guided, favoured and among the best. These words are given by Allah Himself, who knows what is in every heart. And the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ is told to follow the guidance of these prophets, which means that the way of Al-Yasa was the same as the way of all the others: to worship Allah alone and to do good.

His name in the Qur'an is like a quiet sign. It reminds us that the chain of prophets is long, that each of them carried the same message, and that many people who did great good are known fully only to Allah.$tk$, $tk$Do good without waiting for your story to be told. Being among the best in the sight of Allah matters more than being known by people.$tk$, $tk$["6:86","38:48"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yunus$tk$, 'en', 21, $tk$Yunus (Jonah)$tk$, $tk$يونس$tk$, $tk$The man of the fish$tk$, $tk$Yunus, peace be upon him, was sent as a messenger to a people who, according to the Qur'an, numbered a hundred thousand or more. The Qur'an also calls him Dhun-Nun, the man of the fish.

His story begins with a departure. Yunus left in anger. The Qur'an does not hide this: it says that he went off thinking that Allah would not put him in difficulty. Prophets are the best of people, and the Qur'an shows us how they return to Allah when they slip.

Yunus boarded a loaded ship. Lots were drawn on board, and he was among those who lost. He was cast into the sea, and a great fish swallowed him. The Qur'an says that at that moment he was to blame.

In the darkness, Yunus called upon his Lord. He declared that there is no god but Allah, glorified Him, and admitted that he had been among the wrongdoers. He made no excuse and blamed no one else. This short prayer is one of the most famous in the Qur'an.

Allah answered him and saved him from his distress, and the Qur'an adds that this is how He saves the believers. It also says that if Yunus had not been one of those who glorify Allah, he would have stayed in the belly of the fish until the Day of Resurrection.

He was cast onto an open shore, sick and weak, and Allah made a gourd plant grow over him. The Qur'an then says that he was sent to his people, that they believed, and that Allah let them enjoy life for a time. The Qur'an points out that the people of Yunus are a special case: they believed in time, and their faith protected them from a humiliating punishment.

The Qur'an does not name the city of Yunus and does not say how long he remained inside the fish, and we do not need to know.

Allah tells the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to be patient and not to be like the companion of the fish when he cried out in distress. He also says that his Lord chose Yunus and placed him among the righteous. His story therefore carries both a warning against impatience and great hope.$tk$, $tk$Do not give up on a duty out of frustration. And if you have slipped, no darkness is too deep: admit your mistake and call on Allah.$tk$, $tk$["37:139-148","21:87-88","10:98","68:48-50"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$zakariyya$tk$, 'en', 22, $tk$Zakariyya (Zechariah)$tk$, $tk$زكريا$tk$, $tk$The servant who never despaired of his prayer$tk$, $tk$Zakariyya, peace be upon him, was a prophet among the Children of Israel. The Qur'an presents him first as the guardian of Maryam, the mother of Isa, peace be upon them. She had been dedicated to the service of Allah, and Zakariyya was the one entrusted with her care.

Each time he came to see her in the place where she prayed, he found food with her. He asked her where it came from. She answered that it was from Allah, who provides for whom He wills without measure.

This answer touched him deeply. Zakariyya was a very old man, and his wife had never been able to have children. Yet, seeing the generosity of Allah towards Maryam, he turned to his Lord there and then and asked for a good child.

The Qur'an reports this prayer with tenderness. Zakariyya called upon his Lord in a low voice. He said that his bones had become weak and that his hair had turned white, but that he had never been disappointed when he prayed to Him. He was worried about those who would come after him, and he asked for an heir who would be pleasing to Allah. He also said: my Lord, do not leave me alone, and You are the best of inheritors.

The answer came while he was standing in prayer. The angels announced to him a son named Yahya, a name that Allah had not given to anyone before. He would be noble, chaste and a prophet from among the righteous.

Zakariyya was amazed: how could he have a son at his age, with a wife who could not bear children? He was told that this is easy for Allah, who does what He wills. He asked for a sign. The sign was that he would not speak to people for three days, except by gestures, although he was in good health. He came out to his people and signalled to them to glorify Allah morning and evening.

The Qur'an says that Allah answered him, gave him Yahya and cured his wife. It then explains why this family was honoured: they hastened to do good deeds, they called upon Allah with hope and fear, and they were humble before Him.$tk$, $tk$Keep asking Allah with hope, whatever your age or situation. The good you see in others can be an invitation to ask for more yourself.$tk$, $tk$["3:37-41","19:2-11","21:89-90"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$yahya$tk$, 'en', 23, $tk$Yahya (John)$tk$, $tk$يحيى$tk$, $tk$Wise from childhood, gentle and pure$tk$, $tk$Yahya, peace be upon him, is the son of Zakariyya, peace be upon him. He was born in answer to the prayer of his elderly father, when nothing seemed to make it possible. Allah Himself chose his name, and the Qur'an says that no one had been given this name before him.

The Qur'an does not tell the events of his life as a story. It gives instead a portrait of his character, in a few verses of Surah Maryam, and this portrait is one of the most beautiful in the Book.

Allah commanded him to hold firmly to the Scripture, and gave him wisdom while he was still a child. Yahya did not wait to grow old to take his faith seriously: from his earliest years, he understood what Allah asked of him and held on to it with strength.

Allah also gave him tenderness from Himself and purity. He feared Allah. He was dutiful to his parents, and he was neither arrogant nor disobedient. In Surah Al Imran, the angels announce him to Zakariyya as one who would confirm a word from Allah, and as noble, chaste and a prophet from among the righteous. Commentators explain that the word from Allah refers to Isa, peace be upon him, whose mission Yahya confirmed.

Elsewhere the Qur'an names Yahya with Zakariyya, Isa and Ilyas, and says that all of them were among the righteous. In the hadith about the Night Journey, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ relates that he met Yahya and Isa together in the second heaven, and that they were cousins (Sahih al-Bukhari, no. 3887).

The portrait ends with a greeting that Allah gives him: peace upon him the day he was born, the day he dies and the day he is raised alive. These are the three moments when a human being is most vulnerable, and Yahya is promised safety in each of them.

The Qur'an does not describe how Yahya called his people or how he died. Accounts of his death exist in other sources, but they are not found in the Qur'an, and we do not present them as certain.

Yahya shows that strength and gentleness go together. He was firm with the Scripture and soft with people, serious in his faith and kind to his parents.$tk$, $tk$You are never too young to take your faith and your values seriously. Be firm about what is right and gentle with people, starting with your parents.$tk$, $tk$["19:12-15","3:39","21:90","6:85"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$isa$tk$, 'en', 24, $tk$Isa (Jesus)$tk$, $tk$عيسى$tk$, $tk$The Messiah, son of Maryam$tk$, $tk$Isa, peace be upon him, is one of the greatest messengers of Allah. The Qur'an calls him the Messiah, son of Maryam (Mary), and gives his mother a unique place: a whole surah bears her name.

Maryam was a pure and devout young woman. One day, when she had withdrawn from her family to worship, an angel appeared to her and announced that she would have a pure son. She was amazed: how could she have a child when no man had touched her? The angel answered that this is easy for Allah, who creates what He wills. When He decides a matter, He only says to it: Be, and it is.

Maryam gave birth alone, near the trunk of a palm tree, in pain and distress. She was comforted: a stream flowed beneath her, and fresh dates fell from the tree when she shook it.

When she returned to her people carrying the child, they accused her. She simply pointed to him. The newborn then spoke, by the permission of Allah. He said that he was the servant of Allah, that Allah had given him the Scripture and made him a prophet, had made him blessed wherever he was, and had enjoined on him prayer, charity and kindness to his mother.

Isa was sent as a messenger to the Children of Israel. He confirmed the Torah that came before him and received the Gospel. Allah supported him with clear signs: by the permission of Allah, he healed the blind and the leper, gave life to the dead, and shaped a bird from clay which became a living bird. The Qur'an repeats each time that these miracles happened by the permission of Allah.

His message was the same as that of all the prophets: Allah is my Lord and your Lord, so worship Him; that is the straight path. His disciples believed in him and declared that they submitted to Allah. At their request, Isa prayed for a table spread with food to be sent down from heaven as a sign.

The Qur'an also says that Isa announced a messenger who would come after him. When his enemies plotted against him, the Qur'an teaches that they neither killed him nor crucified him, although it appeared so to them, and that Allah raised him to Himself.

Muslims love and honour Isa and his mother.$tk$, $tk$Honour your mother and stand by the truth with gentleness. True greatness lies in being a faithful servant of Allah and in bringing good wherever you are.$tk$, $tk$["3:45-51","19:16-36","5:110-115","4:157-158","61:6"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.prophets (slug, lang, ord, name, name_ar, title, story, lesson, refs) values ($tk$muhammad$tk$, 'en', 25, $tk$Muhammad ﷺ$tk$, $tk$محمد$tk$, $tk$The Seal of the Prophets, a mercy to the worlds$tk$, $tk$The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca around the year 570 CE, in the tribe of Quraysh. His father died before his birth and his mother when he was a small child, so he was raised by his grandfather, then by his uncle Abu Talib. As a young man, he was so honest that people called him al-Amin, the trustworthy. He married Khadija, may Allah be pleased with her, a respected merchant of Mecca.

He used to withdraw to the cave of Hira, on a mountain near Mecca, to reflect and to worship. There, when he was about forty years old, the angel Jibril (Gabriel) brought him the first words of the Qur'an, commanding him to read in the name of his Lord who created. Khadija was the first to believe in him.

For thirteen years he called the people of Mecca to worship Allah alone, to be honest, to care for orphans and the poor, and to give up idols. A few people followed him, but the leaders of Quraysh opposed him, and the believers suffered insults, boycott and persecution. He answered with patience.

In 622, he emigrated to the city of Yathrib, which became Medina. This journey, the Hijra, marks the beginning of the Islamic calendar. The Qur'an recalls how, hidden in a cave with his companion Abu Bakr, he told him not to grieve, for Allah was with them.

In Medina he built a mosque, joined the emigrants from Mecca and the people of Medina in brotherhood, and organised the life of a community. The Muslims had to defend themselves several times, and the Prophet ﷺ also concluded a truce with Quraysh. When it was broken, he returned to Mecca in the year 8 of the Hijra, almost without fighting. He forgave those who had fought him for years and removed the idols from the Kaaba. People then entered the religion of Allah in crowds.

In the year 10, he performed his farewell pilgrimage. At Arafat, he spoke to a great crowd: he reminded them that life, property and honour are sacred, called for the good treatment of women, and declared that old blood feuds and usury were abolished. It was during this pilgrimage that the verse announcing that the religion had been perfected was revealed.

He died in Medina in the year 11 of the Hijra, 632 CE. The Qur'an calls him the Messenger of Allah and the last of the prophets, sent as a mercy to the worlds.$tk$, $tk$Be trustworthy before you speak about faith: character opens hearts. When you have the upper hand, choose mercy and forgiveness.$tk$, $tk$["96:1-5","9:40","110:1-3","5:3","33:40","21:107"]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (slug, lang) do update set ord = excluded.ord, name = excluded.name, name_ar = excluded.name_ar, title = excluded.title, story = excluded.story, lesson = excluded.lesson, refs = excluded.refs;
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('hajj', 'en', $tk${"umrah":{"title":"Umrah step by step","intro":"Umrah is the visit to the Sacred House in Makkah. It can be performed at almost any time of the year and takes only a few hours. It is made of four acts, always in the same order: entering ihram at the miqat, tawaf around the Kaaba, sa'y between Safa and Marwa, then shaving or trimming the hair.\n\nThis guide is a summary. It does not replace a qualified teacher, nor the instructions of the authorities on site, which you should always follow. Where the schools of law differ on a detail, we give the common practice: ask a scholar you trust for your case.","chapters":[{"id":"preparation","title":"Before you leave","body":"A good journey starts at home. Begin with your intention: you are travelling for Allah alone, not to be seen or praised. Repent sincerely, pay back your debts as far as you can, and ask forgiveness from the people you may have wronged. Pay for the trip with lawful money.\n\nThen learn the rites before you go. A pilgrim who knows what to do is calm, and can give his heart to worship instead of worrying. Read this guide several times and, if possible, attend a class with a teacher.\n\nFinally, prepare your body and your papers: walk regularly in the weeks before, check your visa, permit and vaccinations, and pack lightly. You will walk a lot, often in heat and in a crowd.","steps":["Make your intention sincere: this journey is for Allah alone.","Repent, settle your debts and ask forgiveness from those around you.","Learn the four acts of Umrah and their order.","Check your passport, visa, permit and required vaccinations.","Find out which miqat is on your route and when you will reach it.","Train by walking and pack light, comfortable things."],"duas":[]},{"id":"miqat-ihram","title":"The miqat and ihram","body":"The miqat is the boundary that a pilgrim must not cross without being in ihram. There are five: Dhul-Hulayfah for those coming from Madinah, al-Juhfah, Qarn al-Manazil, Yalamlam and Dhat Irq. On a plane, the crew usually announces the miqat: be ready before that moment.\n\nIt is recommended to take a full bath (ghusl) before ihram. Men wear two unstitched cloths, one around the waist and one over the shoulders; women wear their ordinary modest clothes. Then make the intention in your heart and say the talbiyah. Men say it aloud, women quietly.\n\nIhram is the state, not the cloth. In it you must not cut hair or nails, use perfume, hunt, contract a marriage or have marital relations. Men do not wear clothes tailored to the body or cover their head; women do not wear the face veil (niqab) or gloves (Sahih al-Bukhari 1838). Avoid quarrels too (Qur'an 2:197).","steps":["Before the miqat, trim your nails and take a bath (ghusl) if you can.","Men: put on the two ihram cloths. Women: keep ordinary modest clothing.","Men may perfume the body, not the cloths, before the intention; never after.","At the miqat, intend Umrah in your heart and say: Labbayka Allahumma 'umrah.","Repeat the talbiyah often on the way to Makkah.","From now on, respect everything that ihram forbids.","Stop the talbiyah when you begin the tawaf."],"duas":[{"ar":"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ","tr":"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa an-ni'mata laka wa al-mulk, la sharika lak","t":"Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Truly all praise and all blessing are Yours, and all sovereignty. You have no partner.","source":"The talbiyah — Sahih al-Bukhari 1549; Sahih Muslim 1184"}]},{"id":"tawaf","title":"Tawaf around the Kaaba","body":"Tawaf is seven circuits around the Kaaba, keeping it on your left. You need to be in a state of ablution. Each circuit begins and ends at the Black Stone: face it, say the takbir, and kiss or touch it only if this is easy; otherwise simply point towards it. Many pilgrims say « Bismillah, Allahu akbar » there, as reported from the companion Ibn Umar. Walk around the semicircular wall (the Hijr), not through it.\n\nIn this arrival tawaf, men uncover the right shoulder (idtiba) and walk briskly with short steps in the first three circuits (raml). Women walk normally.\n\nThere is no fixed obligatory supplication for each circuit: you may make any dua, in any language, remember Allah or recite the Qur'an. Only between the Yemeni corner and the Black Stone is a precise supplication reported: Qur'an 2:201. Afterwards, pray two rak'at behind Maqam Ibrahim (Qur'an 2:125), then drink Zamzam water.","steps":["Make sure you have ablution. Men uncover the right shoulder (idtiba).","Start level with the Black Stone: face it, say the takbir, point to it if you cannot reach it.","Walk seven circuits with the Kaaba on your left, passing outside the Hijr.","Men walk briskly in the first three circuits (raml), then normally.","Touch the Yemeni corner if it is easy; between it and the Black Stone recite Qur'an 2:201.","During the circuits, make any dua you wish.","After the seventh circuit, men cover the shoulder again; pray two rak'at behind Maqam Ibrahim, or anywhere in the mosque if it is crowded.","Drink Zamzam water and make dua."],"duas":[{"ar":"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ","tr":"Allahu akbar","t":"Allah is the Greatest.","source":"Takbir when level with the Black Stone — Sahih al-Bukhari 1613"},{"ar":"رَبَّنَآ ءَاتِنَا فِي ٱلدُّنۡيَا حَسَنَةࣰ وَفِي ٱلۡأٓخِرَةِ حَسَنَةࣰ وَقِنَا عَذَابَ ٱلنَّارِ","tr":"Rabbana atina fi ad-dunya hasanatan wa fi al-akhirati hasanatan wa qina 'adhab an-nar","t":"Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter, and protect us from the punishment of the Fire.","source":"Qur'an 2:201 — recited between the Yemeni corner and the Black Stone: Sunan Abi Dawud 1892 (hasan)"}]},{"id":"say","title":"Sa'y between Safa and Marwa","body":"After the tawaf, go to Safa. Sa'y is seven legs between the two hills: from Safa to Marwa is one leg, from Marwa back to Safa is the second, and so on. You therefore finish at Marwa.\n\nWhen approaching Safa for the first time, recite the opening of Qur'an 2:158, as the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ did (Sahih Muslim 1218). Climb a little, face the Kaaba, raise your hands, proclaim the greatness and oneness of Allah with the words below, then make your own dua. This is done three times. Do the same at Marwa on each arrival.\n\nBetween the two green lights, men quicken their pace; women walk normally. As in tawaf, no particular supplication is imposed for each leg. Ablution is recommended, and the elderly or ill may use a wheelchair.","steps":["Go to Safa and recite the opening of Qur'an 2:158 as you approach, this first time only.","On Safa, face the Kaaba, raise your hands, say the dhikr of Safa and make dua, three times.","Walk to Marwa; men quicken their pace between the green lights.","On Marwa, face the Kaaba and repeat the dhikr and dua.","Go back to Safa: that is the second leg.","Continue until the seventh leg, which ends at Marwa."],"duas":[{"ar":"إِنَّ ٱلصَّفَا وَٱلۡمَرۡوَةَ مِن شَعَآئِرِ ٱللَّهِ","tr":"Inna as-Safa wa al-Marwata min sha'a'iri Allah","t":"Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah.","source":"Qur'an 2:158 (opening words) — recited on approaching Safa: Sahih Muslim 1218"},{"ar":"لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَهُ لَهُ الْمُلْكُ وَلَهُ الْحَمْدُ وَهُوَ عَلَى كُلِّ شَىْءٍ قَدِيرٌ لاَ إِلَهَ إِلاَّ اللَّهُ وَحْدَهُ أَنْجَزَ وَعْدَهُ وَنَصَرَ عَبْدَهُ وَهَزَمَ الأَحْزَابَ وَحْدَهُ","tr":"La ilaha illa Allahu wahdahu la sharika lah, lahu al-mulku wa lahu al-hamd, wa huwa 'ala kulli shay'in qadir. La ilaha illa Allahu wahdah, anjaza wa'dah, wa nasara 'abdah, wa hazama al-ahzaba wahdah","t":"There is no god but Allah alone, He has no partner. His is the sovereignty and His is the praise, and He has power over all things. There is no god but Allah alone; He fulfilled His promise, gave victory to His servant and defeated the confederates alone.","source":"Dhikr on Safa and Marwa — Sahih Muslim 1218"}]},{"id":"halq-taqsir","title":"Shaving or trimming the hair","body":"The last act of Umrah is to shave the head (halq) or shorten the hair (taqsir). For men, shaving the whole head is better; if they trim instead, the common practice is to shorten the hair of the whole head, not just a few strands. Women never shave: they gather their hair and cut from its end about the length of a fingertip.\n\nA man who performs Umrah shortly before Hajj (tamattu) often chooses to trim now, so that hair remains to be shaved on the tenth day.\n\nUse the barbers outside the mosque rather than cutting hair in the sa'y area, and women should cut their hair in private. With this act the Umrah is complete, and all the restrictions of ihram come to an end.","steps":["Leave the sa'y area once the seventh leg is finished.","Men: shave the head, or shorten the hair of the whole head.","Women: cut a fingertip's length from the end of the hair, in private.","You are now out of ihram: your Umrah is complete."],"duas":[]},{"id":"after","title":"After the Umrah","body":"Your Umrah is finished: thank Allah, who allowed you to reach His House. Everything that ihram had forbidden is permitted again, and you may put on your usual clothes.\n\nMake good use of your remaining days in Makkah. Pray the five prayers in the Sacred Mosque as often as you can, perform extra tawaf when it is not too crowded, read the Qur'an and make dua for yourself, your family and all Muslims. Be gentle with other pilgrims and patient with tiredness and crowds.\n\nMany pilgrims also travel to Madinah to pray in the Prophet's Mosque. This visit is a blessed deed in itself, but it is not a part of Umrah. Back home, try to keep the good habits you gained there.","steps":["Thank Allah and change back into your usual clothes.","Pray in the Sacred Mosque as often as you can.","Perform extra tawaf, read the Qur'an and make much dua.","Keep the good habits of the journey once you are home."],"duas":[]}]},"hajj":{"title":"Hajj day by day","intro":"Hajj is the fifth pillar of Islam. It is an obligation once in a lifetime for every adult Muslim who is able to make the journey (Qur'an 3:97). Its main rites take place from the 8th to the 13th of Dhul-Hijjah, between Makkah, Mina, Arafat and Muzdalifah.\n\nThere are three ways to perform it. In tamattu, you perform a full Umrah during the months of Hajj, leave ihram, then enter ihram again for Hajj on the 8th. In qiran, you join Umrah and Hajj in a single ihram, without leaving it in between. In ifrad, you enter ihram for Hajj alone. A sacrifice is due for tamattu and qiran (Qur'an 2:196), not for ifrad. All three are valid; scholars differ on which is best, and most pilgrims coming from far away perform tamattu.\n\nThis guide is a summary. It does not replace a qualified teacher, nor the instructions of the authorities on site and of your group, which organise movements and timings for everyone's safety. Where the schools of law differ on a detail, we give the common practice: ask a scholar you trust for your case.","chapters":[{"id":"day-8-tarwiyah","title":"8 Dhul-Hijjah: the day of Tarwiyah, in Mina","body":"The Hajj begins on the 8th of Dhul-Hijjah, called the day of Tarwiyah. The pilgrim who is performing tamattu enters ihram again, this time for Hajj, from the place where he is staying in Makkah: bath, ihram garments, intention, then the talbiyah. Those performing qiran or ifrad are still in ihram since the miqat.\n\nEveryone then goes to Mina, a valley of tents a few kilometres from Makkah. There you pray Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and then Fajr of the 9th, each at its own time; the four-rak'at prayers are shortened to two, without being combined.\n\nThis day and night in Mina are a sunnah and a time of preparation. Rest, repeat the talbiyah, read the Qur'an and get your heart ready for tomorrow, the greatest day of the Hajj.","steps":["Tamattu: take a bath and put on ihram where you are staying in Makkah.","Intend Hajj in your heart and say: Labbayka Allahumma hajjan.","Go to Mina with your group, repeating the talbiyah.","Pray Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha in Mina, each at its time, the four-rak'at prayers shortened.","Spend the night in Mina and pray Fajr there."],"duas":[{"ar":"لَبَّيْكَ اللَّهُمَّ لَبَّيْكَ، لَبَّيْكَ لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ لَبَّيْكَ، إِنَّ الْحَمْدَ وَالنِّعْمَةَ لَكَ وَالْمُلْكَ، لاَ شَرِيكَ لَكَ","tr":"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wa an-ni'mata laka wa al-mulk, la sharika lak","t":"Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Truly all praise and all blessing are Yours, and all sovereignty. You have no partner.","source":"The talbiyah — Sahih al-Bukhari 1549; Sahih Muslim 1184"}]},{"id":"day-9-arafat","title":"9 Dhul-Hijjah: Arafat, then Muzdalifah","body":"Standing at Arafat is the pillar of Hajj: whoever misses it has missed the Hajj. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught that the Hajj is Arafat (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 889).\n\nAfter sunrise, leave Mina for Arafat and check that you are inside its boundaries, marked by signs. After the sermon, Dhuhr and Asr are prayed together and shortened, at the time of Dhuhr. Then comes the heart of the Hajj: from midday until sunset, face the qiblah and call upon Allah, ask forgiveness, and pray for those you love. There is no imposed text. Do not leave before sunset.\n\nAfter sunset, go calmly to Muzdalifah. There you pray Maghrib and Isha together, Isha shortened, then you rest for the night in the open air. You may gather there the small pebbles for the stoning.","steps":["After sunrise, go from Mina to Arafat, repeating the talbiyah.","Check that you are inside the boundaries of Arafat.","Pray Dhuhr and Asr combined and shortened at the time of Dhuhr.","Spend the afternoon in dua, dhikr and repentance, facing the qiblah.","Stay in Arafat until the sun has fully set.","Go calmly to Muzdalifah and pray Maghrib and Isha combined there.","Gather small pebbles, the size of a chickpea, then sleep.","Pray Fajr, make dua facing the qiblah, and leave for Mina before sunrise."],"duas":[]},{"id":"day-10-nahr","title":"10 Dhul-Hijjah: the day of sacrifice","body":"The 10th is the day of Eid and the busiest day of the Hajj. Four acts are performed, ideally in this order. First, in Mina, throw seven pebbles one by one at Jamrat al-Aqaba, the largest pillar, saying the takbir with each; the talbiyah stops here (Sahih al-Bukhari 1685). Second, the sacrifice, due for tamattu and qiran, often arranged through an official voucher. Third, men shave or trim and women cut a fingertip's length. Fourth, in Makkah, tawaf al-ifadah, a pillar of the Hajj, followed by the sa'y of Hajj for those who still owe it.\n\nAfter the stoning and the shaving, the restrictions of ihram are lifted, except marital relations, which become lawful after tawaf al-ifadah. The schools differ on the details: ask a scholar you trust for your case.\n\nIf the order changes, there is no harm: the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said so to those who asked that day (Sahih al-Bukhari 83).","steps":["In Mina, throw seven pebbles one by one at Jamrat al-Aqaba, saying Allahu akbar with each.","Stop the talbiyah when you begin the stoning.","Offer the sacrifice, or make sure it is done on your behalf (tamattu and qiran).","Men shave or trim the whole head; women cut a fingertip's length.","Change into ordinary clothes: all is permitted again except marital relations.","Go to Makkah for tawaf al-ifadah, without idtiba or raml, and pray two rak'at.","Perform the sa'y of Hajj if you still owe it.","Return to Mina to spend the night there."],"duas":[{"ar":"اللَّهُ أَكْبَرُ","tr":"Allahu akbar","t":"Allah is the Greatest.","source":"Takbir with each pebble — Sahih al-Bukhari 1751"}]},{"id":"days-11-13-tashriq","title":"11–13 Dhul-Hijjah: the days of Tashriq","body":"The days of Tashriq are spent in Mina. They are days of remembering Allah (Qur'an 2:203), and the nights are spent there too.\n\nEach day, after midday, you stone the three jamarat in order: the small one, the middle one, then Jamrat al-Aqaba, with seven pebbles each, saying the takbir with every throw. After the first and after the second, step aside, face the qiblah and make a long dua with raised hands; after the third, leave without stopping (Sahih al-Bukhari 1751).\n\nYou may leave Mina on the 12th after the stoning, before sunset; whoever stays stones again on the 13th. Both are allowed (Qur'an 2:203). Follow the timetable given to your group. If you are weak or ill, or about the exact time of stoning, ask a scholar you trust for your case.","steps":["Spend the nights of the 11th and 12th in Mina.","Each day after midday, take 21 pebbles to the jamarat.","Stone the small jamrah with seven pebbles, then step aside and make dua.","Stone the middle jamrah with seven pebbles, then step aside and make dua.","Stone Jamrat al-Aqaba with seven pebbles and leave without stopping.","On the 12th, leave Mina before sunset if you wish to leave early.","Otherwise, stay the night and stone the three jamarat again on the 13th."],"duas":[]},{"id":"farewell-tawaf","title":"The farewell tawaf","body":"When all the rites are finished and you are about to leave Makkah, the last thing you do is the farewell tawaf (tawaf al-wada). People were commanded to make the House their last stop; only the woman in her menses was excused from it (Sahih al-Bukhari 1755; Sahih Muslim 1328).\n\nIt is an ordinary tawaf of seven circuits in everyday clothes, without idtiba or raml, followed by two rak'at. There is no sa'y after it. Do it once your luggage is ready and your departure is near; a short wait for transport or for your companions does no harm.\n\nLeave the Sacred Mosque with a grateful heart. Ask Allah to accept your Hajj, to forgive you, and to let you come back. Then try to return home a better person than the one who left.","steps":["Finish all your rites and prepare your luggage first.","Perform seven circuits around the Kaaba in ordinary clothes.","Pray two rak'at after the tawaf.","Make dua that Allah accepts your Hajj, then leave Makkah without delay.","A woman in her menses leaves without this tawaf."],"duas":[]}]},"mistakes":[{"title":"Pushing to kiss the Black Stone","body":"Kissing the Black Stone is a sunnah; harming a Muslim is forbidden. If the crowd is dense, simply point towards the Stone and say the takbir: your tawaf is complete and nobody gets hurt."},{"title":"Believing each circuit has its own required dua","body":"Some booklets give one supplication per circuit. Nothing of the kind is required: there is no fixed obligatory dua for the circuits of tawaf or the legs of sa'y. Speak to Allah with your own words, in your own language."},{"title":"Leaving Arafat before sunset","body":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ stayed at Arafat until the sun had set (Sahih Muslim 1218). Do not rush to the buses in the late afternoon: these are the most precious hours of the whole Hajj."},{"title":"Climbing Jabal ar-Rahmah thinking it is required","body":"The whole plain of Arafat is a place of standing. Climbing the hill is not part of the Hajj and brings no extra reward; the crush there is tiring and dangerous. Stay where you can pray in peace."},{"title":"Standing outside the boundaries of Arafat","body":"Some pilgrims settle just outside Arafat without noticing. Since the standing is the pillar of the Hajj, look for the boundary signs and ask your guide if you are in any doubt."},{"title":"Collecting pebbles that are too large","body":"The pebbles are small, about the size of a chickpea. Big stones, shoes or other objects are not part of the rite and can injure people. Stoning is an act of obedience to Allah, done calmly, not an outburst of anger."},{"title":"Photographing instead of worshipping","body":"A souvenir photo takes a few seconds; filming the whole tawaf or posting live from Arafat takes your heart away. Put the phone away during the rites and protect the sincerity of your intention."},{"title":"Neglecting the miqat when flying","body":"The plane crosses the miqat in the air. Put on the ihram cloths before boarding or early in the flight, and make the intention when the miqat is announced, or a little before. If you passed it without ihram, ask a scholar you trust for your case."},{"title":"Keeping the right shoulder uncovered all the time","body":"Idtiba is only for men and only during the arrival tawaf. Before it and after it, including during prayer and sa'y, both shoulders are covered."},{"title":"Counting a round trip as one leg of sa'y","body":"Safa to Marwa is one leg and Marwa to Safa is another. Sa'y is seven legs and ends at Marwa. Counting each round trip as one would mean walking fourteen legs."},{"title":"Insisting on praying right behind Maqam Ibrahim","body":"Praying in the path of those making tawaf blocks them and puts you at risk. When it is crowded, the two rak'at can be prayed anywhere in the mosque."},{"title":"Arguing and losing patience","body":"Heat, queues and tiredness test everyone. The Qur'an forbids obscenity, wrongdoing and quarrelling during Hajj (2:197). Give way, smile, forgive: good character is part of your pilgrimage."}],"checklist":[{"id":"passport","group":"documents","label":"Passport valid for at least six months"},{"id":"visa-permit","group":"documents","label":"Hajj or Umrah visa and official permit"},{"id":"tickets-bookings","group":"documents","label":"Flight tickets and accommodation bookings"},{"id":"id-copies","group":"documents","label":"Paper and digital copies of all documents"},{"id":"emergency-contacts","group":"documents","label":"Card with your group, hotel and emergency contacts"},{"id":"money-card","group":"documents","label":"Some local cash and a bank card"},{"id":"ihram-garments","group":"clothing","label":"Men: two sets of ihram cloths"},{"id":"modest-clothing","group":"clothing","label":"Women: loose, modest, comfortable clothing"},{"id":"sandals","group":"clothing","label":"Comfortable sandals already worn in"},{"id":"ihram-belt","group":"clothing","label":"Ihram belt or pouch for money and papers"},{"id":"small-bag","group":"clothing","label":"Small bag for shoes and pebbles"},{"id":"umbrella","group":"clothing","label":"Light-coloured umbrella against the sun"},{"id":"vaccinations","group":"health","label":"Required vaccinations and certificate"},{"id":"medication","group":"health","label":"Usual medication with prescription"},{"id":"unscented-toiletries","group":"health","label":"Unscented soap and toiletries for ihram"},{"id":"water-bottle","group":"health","label":"Reusable water bottle"},{"id":"masks-sanitizer","group":"health","label":"Face masks and unscented hand sanitiser"},{"id":"first-aid","group":"health","label":"Small first-aid kit with blister plasters"},{"id":"sincere-intention","group":"spiritual","label":"Renew your intention: for Allah alone"},{"id":"repentance-debts","group":"spiritual","label":"Repent, settle debts and ask forgiveness"},{"id":"learn-rites","group":"spiritual","label":"Learn the rites and their order"},{"id":"talbiyah-memorised","group":"spiritual","label":"Memorise the talbiyah"},{"id":"dua-list","group":"spiritual","label":"Write a list of personal duas for Arafat"},{"id":"quran-dhikr","group":"spiritual","label":"Pocket Qur'an or app for reading and dhikr"}]}$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('quiz', 'en', $tk$[{"id":"q001","cat":"prophets","q":"According to the Qur'an, who was the first human being created by God?","options":["Nuh (Noah)","Adam","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Idris"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adam (peace be upon him) is the first human being and the father of humankind. The Qur'an tells of his creation in 2:30-37."},{"id":"q002","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) built the ark by God's command?","options":["Musa (Moses)","Yunus (Jonah)","Nuh (Noah)","Hud"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nuh (peace be upon him) was ordered to build the ark before the flood, as told in the Qur'an (11:37)."},{"id":"q003","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was swallowed by a great fish?","options":["Ayyub (Job)","Yusuf (Joseph)","Ilyas (Elijah)","Yunus (Jonah)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Yunus (peace be upon him) was swallowed by a great fish, called on God from the darkness and was saved (37:139-144)."},{"id":"q004","cat":"prophets","q":"Which son helped Ibrahim (peace be upon him) raise the foundations of the Kaaba?","options":["Ishaq (Isaac)","Yaqub (Jacob)","Ismail (Ishmael)","Lut (Lot)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Qur'an says that Ibrahim and Ismail (peace be upon them) raised the foundations of the House together (2:127)."},{"id":"q005","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was thrown into a well by his brothers when he was a child?","options":["Yahya (John)","Yusuf (Joseph)","Harun (Aaron)","Dawud (David)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Yusuf (peace be upon him) was thrown into a well by his jealous brothers. His story fills Surah Yusuf, the 12th surah (12:15)."},{"id":"q006","cat":"prophets","q":"To which prophet (peace be upon him) was the Tawrat (Torah) revealed?","options":["Isa (Jesus)","Dawud (David)","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Musa (Moses)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Tawrat was given to Musa (peace be upon him), who led the Children of Israel out of Egypt."},{"id":"q007","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) received the Zabur (Psalms)?","options":["Dawud (David)","Sulayman (Solomon)","Musa (Moses)","Zakariyya (Zechariah)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an states that God gave the Zabur to Dawud (peace be upon him) (4:163 and 17:55)."},{"id":"q008","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) received the Injil (Gospel)?","options":["Isa (Jesus)","Yahya (John)","Musa (Moses)","Yunus (Jonah)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Injil was given to Isa, son of Maryam (peace be upon them), as the Qur'an says in 5:46."},{"id":"q009","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet was the brother of Musa (peace be upon him) and helped him in his mission?","options":["Yusha (Joshua)","Shuayb","Ilyas (Elijah)","Harun (Aaron)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Musa asked God to give him his brother Harun as a helper (20:29-32). Peace be upon them both."},{"id":"q010","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him), tested by a long hardship, is famous for his patience and for calling on his Lord as 'the Most Merciful of the merciful'?","options":["Ayyub (Job)","Harun (Aaron)","Lut (Lot)","Salih"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ayyub (peace be upon him) stayed patient through his hardship and called on his Lord, who relieved him (21:83-84)."},{"id":"q011","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was sent to the people of 'Ad?","options":["Salih","Shuayb","Hud","Lut (Lot)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Hud (peace be upon him) was sent to the people of 'Ad to call them to worship God alone (7:65)."},{"id":"q012","cat":"prophets","q":"The she-camel given as a sign to the people of Thamud is linked to which prophet (peace be upon him)?","options":["Hud","Salih","Nuh (Noah)","Yunus (Jonah)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Salih (peace be upon him) was sent to Thamud, and the she-camel was a sign from God for them (7:73)."},{"id":"q013","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was thrown into a fire by his people, and God made it cool and safe for him?","options":["Musa (Moses)","Ibrahim (Abraham)","Yahya (John)","Hud"],"answer":1,"explain":"When his people threw Ibrahim (peace be upon him) into the fire, God ordered it to be coolness and safety for him (21:68-69)."},{"id":"q014","cat":"prophets","q":"According to the Qur'an, which prophet (peace be upon him) understood the words of an ant warning the other ants?","options":["Yunus (Jonah)","Isa (Jesus)","Sulayman (Solomon)","Yusuf (Joseph)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sulayman (peace be upon him) heard an ant warn the others as his army came near, and he smiled and thanked God (27:18-19)."},{"id":"q015","cat":"prophets","q":"Which prophet (peace be upon him) was the father of Yahya (John)?","options":["Imran","Yaqub (Jacob)","Ayyub (Job)","Zakariyya (Zechariah)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zakariyya (peace be upon him) prayed for a child in his old age and was given the good news of Yahya (19:7)."},{"id":"q016","cat":"seerah","q":"In which city was the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ born?","options":["Medina","Mecca","Ta'if","Jerusalem"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca, in the tribe of Quraysh."},{"id":"q017","cat":"seerah","q":"Where did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ receive the first revelation?","options":["In the cave of Thawr","In the cave of Hira","On Mount Uhud","On the plain of Arafat"],"answer":1,"explain":"The first revelation came in the cave of Hira, on the Mountain of Light near Mecca, where he used to retreat."},{"id":"q018","cat":"seerah","q":"Which angel brought the revelation to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Mikail (Michael)","Jibril (Gabriel)","Israfil","Malik"],"answer":1,"explain":"The angel Jibril (peace be upon him) brought the Qur'an to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ."},{"id":"q019","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the first word of the Qur'an revealed to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Qul (Say)","Qum (Arise)","Iqra (Read)","Sabbih (Glorify)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The first revelation begins with the word 'Iqra', meaning read or recite (96:1)."},{"id":"q020","cat":"seerah","q":"Who was the first wife of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Khadija bint Khuwaylid","Aisha bint Abi Bakr","Hafsa bint Umar","Sawda bint Zam'a"],"answer":0,"explain":"Khadija, a respected merchant of Mecca, was his first wife. He married no other woman while she was alive."},{"id":"q021","cat":"seerah","q":"In which year of the common era did the Hijra, the migration from Mecca to Medina, take place?","options":["622","570","610","632"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Hijra took place in 622 CE. That year later became year 1 of the Islamic calendar."},{"id":"q022","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the city of Medina called before the Hijra?","options":["Khaybar","Tabuk","Ta'if","Yathrib"],"answer":3,"explain":"The city was called Yathrib. After the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ settled there it became known as Medina, 'the City'."},{"id":"q023","cat":"seerah","q":"In which cave did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ take shelter during the Hijra?","options":["The cave of Thawr","The cave of Hira","A cave on Mount Uhud","A cave on Mount Safa"],"answer":0,"explain":"He hid in the cave of Thawr with his companion Abu Bakr while Quraysh searched for them. The Qur'an mentions this moment in 9:40."},{"id":"q024","cat":"seerah","q":"In which year of the Hijra did the Battle of Badr take place?","options":["Year 2","Year 1","Year 5","Year 8"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Battle of Badr took place in Ramadan of the year 2 AH. It was the first major battle of the Muslims."},{"id":"q025","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the name of the grandfather who took care of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ after his mother died?","options":["Abu Lahab","Abu Sufyan","Abd al-Muttalib","Waraqa ibn Nawfal"],"answer":2,"explain":"His grandfather Abd al-Muttalib, a leader of Mecca, looked after him. After the grandfather's death, his uncle Abu Talib took him in."},{"id":"q026","cat":"seerah","q":"What was the name of the mother of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Halima as-Sa'diyya","Fatima bint Asad","Umm Ayman","Amina bint Wahb"],"answer":3,"explain":"His mother was Amina bint Wahb. She died when he was still a small child. Halima as-Sa'diyya was his wet nurse."},{"id":"q027","cat":"seerah","q":"To which tribe did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ belong?","options":["Aws","Khazraj","Thaqif","Quraysh"],"answer":3,"explain":"He belonged to Quraysh, the tribe of Mecca, in the clan of Banu Hashim."},{"id":"q028","cat":"seerah","q":"What is the name of the truce agreed between the Muslims and Quraysh in the year 6 AH?","options":["The Pledge of Aqaba","The Charter of Medina","The Treaty of Hudaybiyya","The Pact of the Virtuous (Hilf al-Fudul)"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Treaty of Hudaybiyya was agreed in 6 AH near Mecca. It brought a period of peace during which Islam spread widely."},{"id":"q029","cat":"seerah","q":"In which year of the Hijra did the Conquest of Mecca take place?","options":["Year 2","Year 4","Year 8","Year 6"],"answer":2,"explain":"The Muslims entered Mecca in the year 8 AH (630 CE). The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ forgave its people."},{"id":"q030","cat":"seerah","q":"Before prophethood, what nickname did the people of Mecca give to the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Al-Hakim (the Wise)","Al-Karim (the Generous)","Ash-Shuja (the Brave)","Al-Amin (the Trustworthy)"],"answer":3,"explain":"He was known as al-Amin, the Trustworthy, because of his honesty. People left their valuables in his care."},{"id":"q031","cat":"companions","q":"Who was the first muezzin of Islam?","options":["Salman al-Farisi","Anas ibn Malik","Bilal ibn Rabah","Zayd ibn Haritha"],"answer":2,"explain":"Bilal ibn Rabah, a freed slave of Abyssinian origin, was chosen by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ to call the adhan."},{"id":"q032","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion travelled with the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ during the Hijra?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Mus'ab ibn Umayr","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Abu Bakr"],"answer":3,"explain":"Abu Bakr was his companion on the journey from Mecca to Medina and stayed with him in the cave of Thawr."},{"id":"q033","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion suggested digging a trench to defend Medina?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari","Salman al-Farisi","Mus'ab ibn Umayr"],"answer":2,"explain":"Salman al-Farisi, who came from Persia, suggested the trench. This is why the battle of 5 AH is called the Battle of the Trench."},{"id":"q034","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion is known by the title 'Sword of God' (Sayf Allah)?","options":["Khalid ibn al-Walid","Bilal ibn Rabah","Zayd ibn Thabit","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":0,"explain":"Khalid ibn al-Walid, a famous commander, is known as Sayf Allah, the Sword of God."},{"id":"q035","cat":"companions","q":"Who was the first person to believe in the message of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ?","options":["Umar ibn al-Khattab","Khadija bint Khuwaylid","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Abu Sufyan"],"answer":1,"explain":"His wife Khadija was the first to believe in him. She comforted and supported him from the very first revelation."},{"id":"q036","cat":"companions","q":"Under which caliph was the Hijri calendar adopted, with the year of the Hijra as its starting point?","options":["Umar ibn al-Khattab","Harun al-Rashid","Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan","Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Hijri calendar was set up during the caliphate of Umar ibn al-Khattab, a few years after the death of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ."},{"id":"q037","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion, a scribe of the revelation, was given the task of gathering the Qur'an into one written collection?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Salman al-Farisi","Zayd ibn Thabit"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zayd ibn Thabit, one of the scribes of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, gathered the written Qur'an into one collection during the caliphate of Abu Bakr."},{"id":"q038","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion was sent to Yathrib before the Hijra to teach Islam to its people?","options":["Mus'ab ibn Umayr","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Anas ibn Malik","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mus'ab ibn Umayr was sent to Yathrib after the first pledge of Aqaba. Many of its people accepted Islam through him."},{"id":"q039","cat":"companions","q":"In which battle was Hamza, the uncle of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, killed?","options":["Badr","The Trench","Hunayn","Uhud"],"answer":3,"explain":"Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib fell as a martyr at the Battle of Uhud, in the year 3 AH."},{"id":"q040","cat":"companions","q":"Who is remembered as the first martyr of Islam?","options":["Umm Ayman","Sumayya bint Khayyat","Halima as-Sa'diyya","Umm Salama"],"answer":1,"explain":"Sumayya bint Khayyat, the mother of Ammar ibn Yasir, was killed in Mecca for refusing to give up her faith."},{"id":"q041","cat":"companions","q":"Which companion spoke for the Muslim emigrants before the Negus, the king of Abyssinia?","options":["Bilal ibn Rabah","Ja'far ibn Abi Talib","Zayd ibn Thabit","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ja'far ibn Abi Talib explained Islam to the Negus, who then let the Muslims live in safety in his land."},{"id":"q042","cat":"companions","q":"Which daughter of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ married Ali ibn Abi Talib?","options":["Fatima","Zaynab","Ruqayya","Umm Kulthum"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fatima married Ali ibn Abi Talib in Medina. They were the parents of al-Hasan and al-Husayn."},{"id":"q043","cat":"companions","q":"What name is given to the people of Medina who welcomed and helped the emigrants from Mecca?","options":["The Muhajirun (Emigrants)","The Quraysh","The Tabi'un (Successors)","The Ansar (Helpers)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Muslims of Medina are called the Ansar, the Helpers. Those who emigrated from Mecca are called the Muhajirun."},{"id":"q044","cat":"companions","q":"Who slept in the bed of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ on the night he left Mecca for the Hijra?","options":["Zayd ibn Haritha","Bilal ibn Rabah","Ali ibn Abi Talib","Anas ibn Malik"],"answer":2,"explain":"The young Ali ibn Abi Talib took his place in the bed that night and stayed behind to return the goods people had entrusted to him."},{"id":"q045","cat":"companions","q":"In whose house did the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ live in Medina while his mosque was being built?","options":["Salman al-Farisi","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Mus'ab ibn Umayr"],"answer":1,"explain":"He stayed in the house of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari until his mosque and his rooms were built."},{"id":"q046","cat":"quran","q":"How many surahs does the Qur'an contain?","options":["114","99","110","120"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an has 114 surahs, from al-Fatiha to an-Nas."},{"id":"q047","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the longest surah of the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Imran","An-Nisa","Ya-Sin","Al-Baqara"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Baqara, the 2nd surah, is the longest in the Qur'an."},{"id":"q048","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the shortest surah of the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Ikhlas","Al-Kawthar","Al-Falaq","Al-Fatiha"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Kawthar, the 108th surah, is the shortest: it has only three short verses."},{"id":"q049","cat":"quran","q":"Which surah opens the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Baqara","Al-Ikhlas","Al-Fatiha","Al-Alaq"],"answer":2,"explain":"Al-Fatiha, 'the Opening', is the first surah of the Qur'an and is recited in every prayer."},{"id":"q050","cat":"quran","q":"Which surah is placed last in the Qur'an?","options":["Al-Falaq","Al-Ikhlas","An-Nas","An-Nasr"],"answer":2,"explain":"An-Nas, the 114th surah, closes the Qur'an."},{"id":"q051","cat":"quran","q":"Into how many parts (juz') is the Qur'an divided?","options":["7","30","60","114"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Qur'an is divided into 30 juz', which makes it easy to read it all in one month."},{"id":"q052","cat":"quran","q":"In which month did the revelation of the Qur'an begin?","options":["Ramadan","Rajab","Muharram","Dhul-Hijja"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an says that it was sent down in the month of Ramadan (2:185)."},{"id":"q053","cat":"quran","q":"In which surah is Ayat al-Kursi, the Verse of the Throne, found?","options":["Al-Imran","Al-Baqara","Al-Kahf","Al-Mulk"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ayat al-Kursi is verse 255 of Surah al-Baqara (2:255)."},{"id":"q054","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the only surah that does not begin with the basmala?","options":["At-Tawba","Al-Anfal","Yunus","Al-Mulk"],"answer":0,"explain":"At-Tawba, the 9th surah, is the only one that does not open with 'In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful'."},{"id":"q055","cat":"quran","q":"In which surah does the basmala appear twice?","options":["An-Nahl","Al-Ankabut","Saba","An-Naml"],"answer":3,"explain":"In Surah an-Naml the basmala comes at the opening and again in the letter of Sulayman, peace be upon him (27:30)."},{"id":"q056","cat":"quran","q":"Which surah of the Qur'an bears the name of the mother of Isa (peace be upon him)?","options":["An-Nisa","Maryam","Al-Imran","An-Nur"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 19th surah is named after Maryam, the mother of Isa (peace be upon them). She is the only woman mentioned by name in the Qur'an."},{"id":"q057","cat":"quran","q":"Over about how many years was the Qur'an revealed?","options":["About 23 years","About 3 years","About 10 years","About 40 years"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Qur'an was revealed little by little over about 23 years, first in Mecca and then in Medina."},{"id":"q058","cat":"quran","q":"How many verses does Surah al-Fatiha have?","options":["3","5","10","7"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Fatiha has seven verses. It is recited in every unit of the prayer."},{"id":"q059","cat":"quran","q":"What are the surahs revealed before the Hijra called?","options":["Medinan (Madani)","Muhkam","Meccan (Makki)","Mufassal"],"answer":2,"explain":"Surahs revealed before the Hijra are called Meccan, and those revealed after it are called Medinan."},{"id":"q060","cat":"quran","q":"Which is the longest verse of the Qur'an?","options":["Ayat al-Kursi (2:255)","The verse of light (24:35)","The verse of debt (2:282)","The first verse of al-Fatiha"],"answer":2,"explain":"The longest verse is verse 282 of al-Baqara. It teaches people to write down their debts and to call witnesses."},{"id":"q061","cat":"history","q":"Which city was the capital of the Umayyad caliphate?","options":["Baghdad","Damascus","Cairo","Medina"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Umayyads ruled from Damascus, in Syria, from 661 to 750."},{"id":"q062","cat":"history","q":"Which Abbasid caliph founded the city of Baghdad in 762?","options":["Harun al-Rashid","Al-Mansur","Al-Ma'mun","Al-Mu'tasim"],"answer":1,"explain":"Caliph al-Mansur founded Baghdad in 762 on the banks of the Tigris. It became one of the greatest cities in the world."},{"id":"q063","cat":"history","q":"Who captured and destroyed Baghdad in 1258?","options":["The Mongols","The Crusaders","The Seljuks","The Ottomans"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Mongol army led by Hulagu took Baghdad in 1258, which ended the Abbasid caliphate of Baghdad."},{"id":"q064","cat":"history","q":"Which Ottoman sultan took Constantinople in 1453?","options":["Mehmed II","Suleiman the Magnificent","Selim I","Osman I"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehmed II, known as 'the Conqueror', took Constantinople in 1453. The city became the Ottoman capital, Istanbul."},{"id":"q065","cat":"history","q":"Who led the Muslim army that crossed from North Africa into the Iberian Peninsula in 711?","options":["Uqba ibn Nafi","Khalid ibn al-Walid","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Tariq ibn Ziyad"],"answer":3,"explain":"Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed the strait in 711. The rock of Gibraltar is named after him: Jabal Tariq, 'the mountain of Tariq'."},{"id":"q066","cat":"history","q":"Who took Jerusalem back from the Crusaders in 1187?","options":["Baybars","Nur ad-Din Zangi","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Mehmed II"],"answer":2,"explain":"Salah ad-Din al-Ayyubi retook Jerusalem in 1187, after his victory at the Battle of Hattin."},{"id":"q067","cat":"history","q":"Which city, the last Muslim kingdom of al-Andalus, fell in 1492?","options":["Cordoba","Granada","Seville","Toledo"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Nasrid kingdom of Granada fell in 1492, ending almost eight centuries of Muslim rule in parts of the Iberian Peninsula."},{"id":"q068","cat":"history","q":"Who defeated the Mongols at the Battle of Ain Jalut in 1260?","options":["The Mamluks of Egypt","The Ottomans","The Seljuks","The Almohads"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Mamluks of Egypt stopped the Mongol advance at Ain Jalut, in Palestine, in 1260."},{"id":"q069","cat":"history","q":"Which dynasty took over the caliphate in 750 and later made Baghdad its capital?","options":["The Ottomans","The Mamluks","The Almoravids","The Abbasids"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Abbasids replaced the Umayyads in 750 and founded Baghdad in 762."},{"id":"q070","cat":"history","q":"Which Umayyad prince fled to al-Andalus and founded the emirate of Cordoba in 756?","options":["Tariq ibn Ziyad","Harun al-Rashid","Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Abd al-Rahman I"],"answer":3,"explain":"Abd al-Rahman I, a survivor of the Umayyad family, reached al-Andalus and founded the emirate of Cordoba in 756."},{"id":"q071","cat":"history","q":"The Ottoman dynasty takes its name from its founder. Who was he?","options":["Mehmed II","Timur (Tamerlane)","Alp Arslan","Osman I"],"answer":3,"explain":"Osman I founded the Ottoman state in Anatolia around 1300. The word 'Ottoman' comes from his name."},{"id":"q072","cat":"history","q":"Who founded the Mughal Empire in India in 1526?","options":["Akbar","Shah Jahan","Babur","Aurangzeb"],"answer":2,"explain":"Babur founded the Mughal Empire after his victory at the Battle of Panipat in 1526."},{"id":"q073","cat":"history","q":"Which 16th-century Ottoman sultan is known as 'the Magnificent' and 'the Lawgiver'?","options":["Mehmed II","Suleiman I","Osman I","Abdulhamid II"],"answer":1,"explain":"Suleiman I ruled from 1520 to 1566, when the Ottoman Empire was at its height. He is called 'Kanuni', the Lawgiver."},{"id":"q074","cat":"history","q":"Mansa Musa, famous for his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324, ruled which empire?","options":["The Songhai Empire","The Ghana Empire","The Mali Empire","The Kanem Empire"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mansa Musa ruled the Mali Empire in West Africa. His pilgrimage of 1324 made the wealth of Mali famous far and wide."},{"id":"q075","cat":"history","q":"Which Seljuk sultan won the Battle of Manzikert against the Byzantines in 1071?","options":["Salah ad-Din (Saladin)","Baybars","Alp Arslan","Osman I"],"answer":2,"explain":"Alp Arslan defeated the Byzantine army at Manzikert in 1071, which opened Anatolia to the Turks."},{"id":"q076","cat":"sciences","q":"Which branch of mathematics takes its name from the book 'al-Jabr' by al-Khwarizmi?","options":["Geometry","Algebra","Trigonometry","Arithmetic"],"answer":1,"explain":"The word algebra comes from 'al-jabr', in the title of the book that al-Khwarizmi wrote in Baghdad in the 9th century."},{"id":"q077","cat":"sciences","q":"The word 'algorithm' comes from the Latin form of which scholar's name?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Kindi","Al-Biruni","Al-Battani"],"answer":0,"explain":"'Algorithm' comes from 'Algoritmi', the Latin form of the name of the mathematician al-Khwarizmi."},{"id":"q078","cat":"sciences","q":"Who wrote the 'Canon of Medicine' (al-Qanun fi al-Tibb)?","options":["Al-Razi (Rhazes)","Al-Zahrawi","Ibn Sina (Avicenna)","Ibn al-Nafis"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ibn Sina wrote the Canon of Medicine in the 11th century. It was used to teach medicine for centuries, in the Muslim world and in Europe."},{"id":"q079","cat":"sciences","q":"Which scholar wrote the 'Book of Optics' and explained that we see because light enters the eye?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Ibn al-Haytham","Ibn Khaldun","Al-Idrisi"],"answer":1,"explain":"Ibn al-Haytham, who lived in the 10th and 11th centuries, wrote the Book of Optics (Kitab al-Manazir) and relied on careful experiments."},{"id":"q080","cat":"sciences","q":"The Andalusian scholar al-Zahrawi is famous as a pioneer in which field?","options":["Astronomy","Surgery","Geography","Algebra"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Zahrawi, who lived near Cordoba in the 10th century, described surgical operations and many instruments in his encyclopedia al-Tasrif."},{"id":"q081","cat":"sciences","q":"Which physician wrote a famous treatise that told smallpox apart from measles?","options":["Al-Kindi","Al-Farabi","Al-Idrisi","Al-Razi (Rhazes)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Razi, a great physician of the 9th and 10th centuries, wrote a treatise on smallpox and measles that carefully describes both illnesses."},{"id":"q082","cat":"sciences","q":"Which 13th-century physician described how blood passes from the heart through the lungs?","options":["Al-Khwarizmi","Al-Biruni","Ibn al-Nafis","Ibn Khaldun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ibn al-Nafis described the pulmonary circulation of the blood in the 13th century, long before it was known in Europe."},{"id":"q083","cat":"sciences","q":"Which 11th-century scholar calculated the radius of the Earth and wrote a major study of India?","options":["Ibn Rushd (Averroes)","Jabir ibn Hayyan","Al-Zahrawi","Al-Biruni"],"answer":3,"explain":"Al-Biruni worked out the radius of the Earth by an ingenious method, with a result close to the modern value, and wrote a detailed book about India."},{"id":"q084","cat":"sciences","q":"Which geographer drew a famous map of the world for King Roger II of Sicily in the 12th century?","options":["Ibn Battuta","Ibn Khaldun","Al-Idrisi","Al-Khwarizmi"],"answer":2,"explain":"Al-Idrisi completed his map of the world and the book that goes with it in 1154, at the court of Roger II in Sicily."},{"id":"q085","cat":"sciences","q":"Jabir ibn Hayyan is especially known for his work in which field?","options":["Optics","Surgery","Chemistry","Geography"],"answer":2,"explain":"Jabir ibn Hayyan is one of the great names of early chemistry (alchemy), with many works attributed to him."},{"id":"q086","cat":"sciences","q":"In which city was the House of Wisdom (Bayt al-Hikma), the famous Abbasid library linked to the translation movement?","options":["Baghdad","Cordoba","Cairo","Damascus"],"answer":0,"explain":"The House of Wisdom was in Abbasid Baghdad, where scholars translated and studied works from Greek, Persian and Indian traditions."},{"id":"q087","cat":"sciences","q":"Who wrote the 'Muqaddima', a famous work on history and society?","options":["Ibn Khaldun","Ibn Battuta","Ibn Rushd (Averroes)","Al-Ghazali"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ibn Khaldun, born in Tunis in 1332, wrote the Muqaddima. He is seen as a forerunner of sociology and of the modern study of history."},{"id":"q088","cat":"sciences","q":"What was the astrolabe, an instrument perfected by Muslim scholars, used for?","options":["Measuring temperature","Weighing gold and silver","Grinding grain","Observing the position of the sun and stars"],"answer":3,"explain":"The astrolabe was used to observe the sun and stars, which helped people tell the time and find directions."},{"id":"q089","cat":"sciences","q":"What is the engineer al-Jazari, who finished his famous book in 1206, known for?","options":["Ingenious machines and water clocks","A map of the world","A treatise on smallpox","A book on algebra"],"answer":0,"explain":"Al-Jazari wrote the Book of Knowledge of Ingenious Mechanical Devices, which describes water clocks, pumps and automata."},{"id":"q090","cat":"sciences","q":"By what Latin name is the Andalusian scholar Ibn Rushd known in Europe?","options":["Avicenna","Alhazen","Rhazes","Averroes"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ibn Rushd of Cordoba is known as Averroes. His commentaries on Aristotle were read widely in medieval Europe."},{"id":"q091","cat":"pillars","q":"How many pillars does Islam have?","options":["Three","Five","Four","Seven"],"answer":1,"explain":"Islam has five pillars: the testimony of faith, prayer, zakat, fasting in Ramadan and the pilgrimage to Mecca."},{"id":"q092","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the first pillar of Islam?","options":["The prayer (salat)","The fast (sawm)","The pilgrimage (hajj)","The testimony of faith (shahada)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first pillar is the shahada: to bear witness that there is no god but God and that Muhammad ﷺ is His Messenger."},{"id":"q093","cat":"pillars","q":"How many obligatory prayers are there each day?","options":["Two","Three","Five","Seven"],"answer":2,"explain":"There are five obligatory prayers each day: Fajr, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib and Isha."},{"id":"q094","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the name of the dawn prayer?","options":["Dhuhr","Fajr","Asr","Isha"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fajr is the dawn prayer, the first of the five daily prayers."},{"id":"q095","cat":"pillars","q":"How many obligatory units (rak'at) does the Fajr prayer have?","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":1,"explain":"The obligatory Fajr prayer has two rak'at."},{"id":"q096","cat":"pillars","q":"Which month of the Islamic calendar is Ramadan?","options":["The ninth","The first","The seventh","The twelfth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ramadan is the ninth month of the Hijri calendar. It is the month of fasting."},{"id":"q097","cat":"pillars","q":"In Ramadan, when does each day's fast begin?","options":["At dawn","At sunrise","At noon","At midnight"],"answer":0,"explain":"The fast begins at dawn, when the first light appears, as the Qur'an explains in 2:187."},{"id":"q098","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the meal that breaks the fast in the evening called?","options":["Suhur","Walima","Aqiqa","Iftar"],"answer":3,"explain":"Iftar is the meal that breaks the fast. Suhur is the meal taken before dawn."},{"id":"q099","cat":"pillars","q":"What is the usual rate of zakat on savings such as money, gold and silver?","options":["2.5%","1%","5%","15%"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zakat on savings is 2.5%, one fortieth, once the minimum amount has been held for a full lunar year."},{"id":"q100","cat":"pillars","q":"In which month does the Hajj take place?","options":["Ramadan","Dhul-Hijja","Muharram","Shawwal"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Hajj takes place in Dhul-Hijja, the twelfth and last month of the Hijri calendar."},{"id":"q101","cat":"pillars","q":"How many times do pilgrims go around the Kaaba in one tawaf?","options":["Three","Five","Seven","Nine"],"answer":2,"explain":"A tawaf is made of seven circuits around the Kaaba."},{"id":"q102","cat":"pillars","q":"Between which two hills do pilgrims walk during the sa'i?","options":["Uhud and Thawr","Arafat and Mina","Safa and Marwa","Hira and Thawr"],"answer":2,"explain":"Pilgrims walk seven times between Safa and Marwa, which the Qur'an calls symbols of God (2:158)."},{"id":"q103","cat":"pillars","q":"On which day of Dhul-Hijja do pilgrims stand at Arafat?","options":["The 9th","The 1st","The 10th","The 13th"],"answer":0,"explain":"The standing at Arafat is on the 9th of Dhul-Hijja. It is the heart of the Hajj."},{"id":"q104","cat":"pillars","q":"How many times in a lifetime is the Hajj required of a Muslim who is able to make it?","options":["Three times","Every year","Every five years","Once"],"answer":3,"explain":"The Hajj is required once in a lifetime for those who are able to make the journey (3:97)."},{"id":"q105","cat":"pillars","q":"Towards which city did the first Muslims pray before the qibla became the Kaaba?","options":["Medina","Damascus","Ta'if","Jerusalem"],"answer":3,"explain":"The first Muslims prayed towards Jerusalem. The qibla was then turned towards the Sacred Mosque in Mecca (2:144)."},{"id":"q106","cat":"civilisation","q":"Who founded the mosque of al-Qarawiyyin in Fez in the 9th century, which grew into a famous university?","options":["Rabia al-Adawiyya","Fatima al-Fihri","Zubayda bint Ja'far","Shajar al-Durr"],"answer":1,"explain":"According to tradition, Fatima al-Fihri founded al-Qarawiyyin in Fez, Morocco, in 859. It is often described as the oldest university still in activity."},{"id":"q107","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city of al-Andalus is the great mosque famous for its forest of columns and its red and white arches?","options":["Seville","Cordoba","Granada","Toledo"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Great Mosque of Cordoba was begun in the 8th century, when Cordoba was the capital of al-Andalus."},{"id":"q108","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city is the Alhambra palace?","options":["Granada","Cordoba","Marrakesh","Fez"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Alhambra stands in Granada, in the south of Spain. It was the palace of the Nasrid rulers."},{"id":"q109","cat":"civilisation","q":"Which Mughal emperor had the Taj Mahal built?","options":["Akbar","Babur","Aurangzeb","Shah Jahan"],"answer":3,"explain":"Shah Jahan had the Taj Mahal built in Agra in the 17th century, in memory of his wife Mumtaz Mahal."},{"id":"q110","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city was the great traveller Ibn Battuta born?","options":["Fez","Tunis","Cairo","Tangier"],"answer":3,"explain":"Ibn Battuta was born in Tangier, Morocco, in 1304. He travelled for nearly thirty years across Africa, Asia and Europe."},{"id":"q111","cat":"civilisation","q":"Timbuktu, a famous centre of learning and manuscripts, is in which present-day country?","options":["Egypt","Morocco","Mali","Sudan"],"answer":2,"explain":"Timbuktu is in Mali. Its mosques and schools drew students and scholars from across West Africa."},{"id":"q112","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city is al-Azhar, one of the oldest universities in the world?","options":["Baghdad","Cairo","Damascus","Kairouan"],"answer":1,"explain":"Al-Azhar was founded in Cairo in the 10th century and is still a major centre of learning today."},{"id":"q113","cat":"civilisation","q":"In which city is the Dome of the Rock?","options":["Jerusalem","Damascus","Medina","Cairo"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Dome of the Rock is in Jerusalem. It was completed at the end of the 7th century under the Umayyad caliph Abd al-Malik."},{"id":"q114","cat":"civilisation","q":"Which Ottoman architect designed the Süleymaniye Mosque in Istanbul?","options":["Al-Jazari","Ulugh Beg","Mimar Sinan","Ibn Khaldun"],"answer":2,"explain":"Mimar Sinan, chief architect of the Ottoman Empire in the 16th century, designed the Süleymaniye in Istanbul and the Selimiye in Edirne."},{"id":"q115","cat":"civilisation","q":"In the medieval Muslim world, what was a 'bimaristan'?","options":["A library","An observatory","A covered market","A hospital"],"answer":3,"explain":"A bimaristan was a hospital. Cities such as Baghdad, Damascus and Cairo had large hospitals where the sick were treated."},{"id":"q116","cat":"civilisation","q":"What is the first month of the Hijri calendar?","options":["Muharram","Ramadan","Rajab","Shawwal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Muharram is the first of the twelve months of the Hijri calendar, which follows the moon."},{"id":"q117","cat":"civilisation","q":"In a mosque, what is the mihrab?","options":["The pulpit used for the Friday sermon","The niche that shows the direction of Mecca","The tower used for the call to prayer","The fountain used for ablutions"],"answer":1,"explain":"The mihrab is a niche in the wall that shows the qibla, the direction of Mecca. The pulpit is called the minbar and the tower the minaret."},{"id":"q118","cat":"civilisation","q":"The Great Mosque of Kairouan, one of the oldest in Africa, is in which country?","options":["Tunisia","Morocco","Egypt","Syria"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kairouan is in Tunisia. Its Great Mosque goes back to the founding of the city by Uqba ibn Nafi in 670."},{"id":"q119","cat":"civilisation","q":"The Kufic script, one of the oldest styles of Arabic calligraphy, is named after which city?","options":["Cordoba","Konya","Kufa","Kabul"],"answer":2,"explain":"Kufic script takes its name from Kufa, in Iraq. Many early copies of the Qur'an were written in this angular style."},{"id":"q120","cat":"civilisation","q":"In Islamic civilisation, what is a 'waqf'?","options":["A trade caravan","A style of calligraphy","A charitable endowment","A tax on harvests"],"answer":2,"explain":"A waqf is property set aside for good, such as a school, a hospital or a fountain. Its income keeps serving people over time."}]$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
insert into public.premium_content (kind, lang, payload) values ('days', 'en', $tk${"new-year":{"detail":"The first day of Muharram opens the Hijri year. The Islamic calendar is lunar: each month begins with the new crescent, so the year is about eleven days shorter than the solar year.\n\nThe calendar was organised under the caliph Umar ibn al-Khattab, some years after the death of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The Companions chose to count the years from the Hijra, the emigration of the Prophet ﷺ from Mecca to Medina in 622 CE, and to keep Muharram as the first month. The Hijra itself took place later in the year, in the month of Rabi' al-Awwal.\n\nMuharram is one of the four sacred months mentioned in the Qur'an (9:36). The Prophet ﷺ taught that the best fast after Ramadan is the fast of Allah's month, Muharram (Sahih Muslim 1163).\n\nNo special prayer or celebration is prescribed for this day. Many Muslims simply use it to remember the Hijra, look back on the past year and make good resolutions.","sources":["Qur'an 9:36","Sahih Muslim 1163"]},"tasua":{"detail":"Tasu'a is the ninth day of Muharram, the day before Ashura.\n\nWhen the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ fasted Ashura and told the Muslims to fast it, his Companions pointed out that Jews and Christians also honoured that day. He answered that the following year, if Allah willed, they would fast the ninth as well. He passed away before the next Muharram came (Sahih Muslim 1134).\n\nFor this reason, scholars recommend fasting the ninth together with the tenth. This gives the Muslim fast its own form while keeping the link with the day on which Allah saved Moses (peace be upon him).\n\nThis fast is voluntary. Someone who can only fast the tenth still fasts Ashura, and nobody who is ill, travelling or unable to fast should feel guilty. Muharram as a whole is a sacred month, and the Prophet ﷺ taught that fasting in it is the best fast after Ramadan (Sahih Muslim 1163).","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1134","Sahih Muslim 1163"]},"ashura":{"detail":"Ashura is the tenth day of Muharram. Its story goes back to Moses (peace be upon him): on this day Allah saved him and the Children of Israel from their enemy.\n\nWhen the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ came to Medina, he found the Jews fasting on this day in memory of that rescue. He said that the Muslims had more right to Moses, so he fasted it and told the Muslims to fast it (Sahih al-Bukhari 2004). When the fast of Ramadan became obligatory, fasting Ashura remained a voluntary, recommended act (Sahih al-Bukhari 2002).\n\nAsked about its reward, the Prophet ﷺ said that it expiates the sins of the past year (Sahih Muslim 1162). It is recommended to fast the ninth day along with it (Sahih Muslim 1134).","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 2004","Sahih al-Bukhari 2002","Sahih Muslim 1162","Sahih Muslim 1134"]},"mawlid":{"detail":"The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ was born in Mecca, in the clan of Banu Hashim of the tribe of Quraysh, in the year known as the Year of the Elephant, around 570 CE. His father Abdullah died before his birth, and his mother Amina died when he was a small child.\n\nIt is established that he was born on a Monday: asked about fasting on Mondays, he said it was the day he was born and the day revelation came to him (Sahih Muslim 1162). Most historians place his birth in the month of Rabi' al-Awwal. The 12th is the best-known date, but other days have been suggested and none is certain.\n\nMuslims differ about marking this day. Many gather to recall his life, send blessings upon him and share food. Others do not single the day out, because the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions did not do so. All agree on loving him and following his example every day; the Qur'an describes him as a mercy to the worlds (21:107).","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1162","Qur'an 21:107"]},"isra-miraj":{"detail":"Al-Isra is the night journey of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ from the Sacred Mosque in Mecca to al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem. The Qur'an mentions it at the opening of Surah al-Isra (17:1). Al-Mi'raj is his ascension from there through the heavens, where he met earlier prophets (peace be upon them) and was shown great signs of his Lord; Surah an-Najm alludes to this (53:13-18).\n\nIt happened in Mecca, before the Hijra, in a period of hardship for the Prophet ﷺ. The most lasting gift of that night is the five daily prayers, which were made obligatory then (Sahih al-Bukhari 349).\n\nThe exact date is not known with certainty. The 27th of Rajab is the date most commonly remembered, but early historians mention other dates.\n\nNo particular prayer or fast is established for this night. Many Muslims use the occasion to retell the event and to remember the value of the prayer; others do not mark the date. Rajab is one of the four sacred months (9:36).","sources":["Qur'an 17:1","Qur'an 53:13-18","Sahih al-Bukhari 349","Qur'an 9:36"]},"mid-shaban":{"detail":"Sha'ban is the eighth month, just before Ramadan. Aisha (may Allah be pleased with her) reported that she never saw the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ fast in any month, apart from Ramadan, as much as he did in Sha'ban (Sahih al-Bukhari 1969). It is a month for getting ready: voluntary fasts, making up fasts missed in the previous Ramadan, and reading the Qur'an.\n\nThe night of the 15th, in the middle of the month, is known in many countries by names such as Laylat al-Bara'a, Shab-e-Barat or Nisfu Sya'ban. Several reports speak of Allah's forgiveness on this night. Scholars differ about how strong these reports are, and so they differ about the practice: many Muslims spend part of the night in prayer and supplication and fast during the day; others treat it like any other night of Sha'ban and do not set aside special worship for it.\n\nWhat is agreed is that asking for forgiveness, mending relations with others and voluntary fasting are good throughout Sha'ban, the doorway to Ramadan.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 1969"]},"ramadan-start":{"detail":"Ramadan is the ninth month of the Hijri year, the month in which the Qur'an was sent down as guidance for people (2:185). Fasting during it is one of the five pillars of Islam (Sahih al-Bukhari 8). It was prescribed in Medina, in the second year after the Hijra.\n\nThe month begins when the new crescent is sighted; if it cannot be seen, Sha'ban is completed as thirty days (Sahih al-Bukhari 1909). This is why the start may differ by a day from one country to another.\n\nFrom dawn to sunset, those who fast give up food, drink and marital relations. The sick and travellers are exempt and make up the days later, and those who cannot fast at all feed a poor person instead (2:184-185). The Qur'an gives the aim of the fast: to grow in God-consciousness (2:183).\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that when Ramadan begins, the gates of heaven are opened, the gates of Hell are closed and the devils are chained (Sahih al-Bukhari 1899). It is a month of prayer, Qur'an and generosity.","sources":["Qur'an 2:183-185","Sahih al-Bukhari 8","Sahih al-Bukhari 1909","Sahih al-Bukhari 1899"]},"last-ten-nights":{"detail":"The last ten nights of Ramadan begin on the evening before the 21st day and run to the end of the month, which has 29 or 30 days. They are the most precious part of Ramadan, because Laylat al-Qadr is among them.\n\nAisha (may Allah be pleased with her) reported that when these ten began, the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ would tighten his belt, spend the night in prayer and wake his family (Sahih al-Bukhari 2024). He strove in worship in these nights more than at any other time (Sahih Muslim 1175).\n\nHe also practised i'tikaf, staying in the mosque to devote himself to worship, during the last ten days of Ramadan until he died (Sahih al-Bukhari 2026).\n\nWhat is recommended: night prayer, reading the Qur'an, plenty of supplication, asking forgiveness and charity. Those who cannot stay up for long can still pray a little each night, so that they do not miss the night that is better than a thousand months (97:3). Zakat al-fitr is also prepared in these days, before the Eid prayer.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 2024","Sahih Muslim 1175","Sahih al-Bukhari 2026","Qur'an 97:3"]},"laylat-al-qadr":{"detail":"Laylat al-Qadr, the Night of Decree, is the night in which the Qur'an was sent down. A whole surah is devoted to it: it is better than a thousand months, the angels and the Spirit descend in it by their Lord's permission, and it is peace until the rising of dawn (97:1-5). The Qur'an also calls it a blessed night (44:3).\n\nIts exact date has been kept hidden. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ told the believers to look for it in the odd nights of the last ten of Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 2017): the 21st, 23rd, 25th, 27th or 29th. The 27th is the night most often mentioned, and many communities gather on it, but it is not certain, and scholars hold different views. The wisest course is to make an effort on all ten nights.\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ said that whoever stands in prayer on Laylat al-Qadr with faith and hoping for reward will have his past sins forgiven (Sahih al-Bukhari 2014). Recommended acts are night prayer, reciting the Qur'an, supplication and asking Allah for pardon.","sources":["Qur'an 97:1-5","Qur'an 44:3","Sahih al-Bukhari 2017","Sahih al-Bukhari 2014"]},"eid-al-fitr":{"detail":"Eid al-Fitr, the Festival of Breaking the Fast, falls on the first day of Shawwal and closes the month of Ramadan. The Qur'an asks the believers to complete the number of days and to glorify Allah for having guided them (2:185).\n\nBefore the Eid prayer, zakat al-fitr is given: a small charity of staple food, due for every member of the household, so that the poor can share in the day. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ ordered it to be paid before people go out to the prayer (Sahih al-Bukhari 1503).\n\nIn the morning, Muslims say the takbir, wear their best clothes and gather for the Eid prayer, which is followed by a sermon (Sahih al-Bukhari 956). The Prophet ﷺ would not leave for the prayer on this day until he had eaten some dates (Sahih al-Bukhari 953).\n\nFasting on the day of Eid is not permitted (Sahih al-Bukhari 1991). It is a day of thanks, family visits and kindness to neighbours. Voluntary fasts, such as the six days of Shawwal, can start from the second day.","sources":["Qur'an 2:185","Sahih al-Bukhari 1503","Sahih al-Bukhari 956","Sahih al-Bukhari 953","Sahih al-Bukhari 1991"]},"six-shawwal":{"detail":"Shawwal is the month that follows Ramadan. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that whoever fasts Ramadan and then follows it with six days of Shawwal, it is as if he had fasted the whole year (Sahih Muslim 1164).\n\nScholars explain this with the rule that a good deed is rewarded ten times over (6:160): the month of Ramadan counts as ten months, and six more days count as two months, which makes a full year.\n\nAny six days of the month count. The app shows the 2nd to the 7th only as a convenient range: the days may be fasted one after another or spread out, at the beginning, middle or end of Shawwal. The only day excluded is the 1st, the day of Eid, when fasting is not permitted.\n\nThis fast is voluntary, not an obligation. Someone who still has days of Ramadan to make up should plan for them too: many scholars advise making them up first, while others allow the six days before. It is a gentle way to keep the good habits of Ramadan alive.","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1164","Qur'an 6:160"]},"dhul-hijja-nine":{"detail":"Dhul-Hijja is the twelfth month, the month of the Hajj. Its first ten days are among the best days of the year: the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that no good deeds are better than those done in these days (Sahih al-Bukhari 969). Many commentators also understand the ten nights by which Allah swears in the Qur'an (89:2) as referring to them.\n\nSince fasting is one of the best deeds, it is recommended to fast during the first nine days, as much as one can. The ninth, the Day of Arafat, is the most emphasised. The tenth is Eid al-Adha, when fasting is not permitted.\n\nOther recommended acts are to repeat the takbir, to praise Allah often, to pray, to give charity and to read the Qur'an. The Qur'an speaks of mentioning Allah's name on known days (22:28).\n\nWhoever intends to offer a sacrifice is told not to cut his hair or nails from the start of the month until the sacrifice is done (Sahih Muslim 1977). During these days, pilgrims from all over the world gather in Mecca.","sources":["Sahih al-Bukhari 969","Qur'an 89:2","Qur'an 22:28","Sahih Muslim 1977"]},"arafat":{"detail":"The ninth of Dhul-Hijja is the Day of Arafat, the heart of the Hajj. From midday until sunset the pilgrims stand on the plain of Arafat, near Mecca, in prayer and supplication. Standing at Arafat is the essential pillar of the pilgrimage.\n\nIt was on this day, a Friday, during the Farewell Pilgrimage of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, that the verse announcing that Allah had perfected the religion was revealed (5:3), as Umar ibn al-Khattab reported (Sahih al-Bukhari 45).\n\nThe Prophet ﷺ said that there is no day on which Allah frees more servants from the Fire than the Day of Arafat (Sahih Muslim 1348).\n\nFor those who are not on Hajj, fasting this day is strongly recommended: the Prophet ﷺ said that it expiates the sins of the previous year and of the coming year (Sahih Muslim 1162). The pilgrims themselves are not asked to fast; the Prophet ﷺ did not fast while standing at Arafat (Sahih al-Bukhari 1988).\n\nIt is a day for supplication, remembrance of Allah and seeking forgiveness, wherever one is.","sources":["Qur'an 5:3","Sahih al-Bukhari 45","Sahih Muslim 1348","Sahih Muslim 1162","Sahih al-Bukhari 1988"]},"eid-al-adha":{"detail":"Eid al-Adha, the Festival of Sacrifice, falls on the tenth of Dhul-Hijja, the day after Arafat. It coincides with the main rites of the Hajj at Mina and Mecca.\n\nIt recalls the trial of Abraham (peace be upon him). He saw in a dream that he was sacrificing his son; both submitted to Allah's command, and Allah ransomed the son with a great sacrifice (37:102-107).\n\nThe day begins with the Eid prayer, followed by a sermon (Sahih al-Bukhari 956). After the prayer, those who are able sacrifice a sheep, a goat, a cow or a camel. The meat is eaten by the family, offered to relatives and neighbours and given to the poor. The Qur'an reminds believers that neither the meat nor the blood reaches Allah; what reaches Him is their piety (22:37).\n\nFasting is not permitted on this day (Sahih al-Bukhari 1991). Muslims say the takbir, wear their best clothes, visit one another and share food. The sacrifice may also be made on the days that follow.","sources":["Qur'an 37:102-107","Sahih al-Bukhari 956","Qur'an 22:37","Sahih al-Bukhari 1991"]},"tashriq":{"detail":"The days of Tashriq are the 11th, 12th and 13th of Dhul-Hijja, the three days after Eid al-Adha. The Prophet Muhammad ﷺ described them as days of eating, drinking and remembrance of Allah (Sahih Muslim 1141).\n\nDuring these days the pilgrims stay at Mina and throw pebbles at the three pillars called the jamarat each day. The Qur'an tells them to remember Allah during these numbered days, and says that there is no sin on whoever hurries to leave after two days, nor on whoever stays longer (2:203). So pilgrims may leave on the 12th or stay until the 13th.\n\nFasting on these days is not permitted. The only exception reported is for pilgrims who owe a sacrificial offering and cannot find one (Sahih al-Bukhari 1997).\n\nFor everyone else, these are days of joy and gratitude: sharing the meat of the sacrifice, visiting relatives and saying the takbir, especially after the daily prayers. The Eid sacrifice may still be offered in these days, with details that vary between the schools of law.","sources":["Sahih Muslim 1141","Qur'an 2:203","Sahih al-Bukhari 1997"]}}$tk$::jsonb)
  on conflict (kind, lang) do update set payload = excluded.payload, updated_at = now();
